export const CHAPTER_CONTENT = {
  // ── MODULE 1 · ADU BASICS ──
  m1c1: [
    { p: "An Accessory Dwelling Unit (ADU) is a legal residential dwelling located on the same property as an existing single-family residence." },
    { h: "An ADU includes", list: ["Kitchen", "Bathroom", "Sleeping area", "Living area", "Utilities", "Private entrance"] },
    { p: "An ADU is designed for independent living and is built in accordance with the regulations established by the municipality where it is located." },
    { p: "Many municipalities limit the maximum size of an ADU to approximately 1,200 square feet or less, although the allowable size varies by location." },
    { p: "Homeowners build ADUs for many reasons, including housing family members, guests, or caregivers; generating rental income where permitted; retirement living; home offices; hobby rooms; and flexible living space." },
    { p: "Throughout this course, we'll use the term ADU because it is the terminology used by municipalities, planning departments, builders, and manufacturers." },
    { remember: "An ADU is a legal residential dwelling designed for independent living that includes a kitchen, bathroom, and sleeping area." },
  ],
  m1c2: [
    { h: "Depending on where you live, an ADU may be called", list: ["Tiny House", "Granny Flat", "Casita", "Backyard Cottage", "Carriage House", "Laneway House", "Guest House"] },
    { p: "Although different names are used throughout the country, municipalities generally use the term Accessory Dwelling Unit (ADU) when publishing regulations, permits, and planning information." },
    { p: "When researching your city's requirements, search using the term ADU." },
    { remember: "Different names describe the same general concept. Your city will generally use the term Accessory Dwelling Unit (ADU)." },
  ],
  m1c3: [
    { p: "An ADU is built on residential property that already contains a primary home." },
    { p: "Where an ADU can be located depends on your property's characteristics and your municipality's regulations." },
    { h: "Factors that determine where an ADU may be built", list: ["Lot dimensions", "Setbacks", "Easements", "Utility locations", "Site access", "Existing structures", "Topography", "Local zoning regulations"] },
    { p: "Every property is unique. Two neighboring homes may have completely different building opportunities because of differences in lot size, easements, utility locations, or local regulations." },
    { p: "Understanding where an ADU can be built is one of the first steps in determining what is possible on your property. Your pre-site budget estimate cannot be completed until you know where the ADU will be placed." },
    { remember: "The first question is not \"Which ADU do I want?\" The first question is \"What can I legally build on my property?\"" },
  ],
  m1c4: [
    { p: "One of the biggest advantages of taking the ADUAtlas course is discovering how many ADU options are available today." },
    { p: "Most homeowners begin by looking at a single style they like without realizing there are so many options. Today's ADU marketplace includes more than 25 construction methods, product types, and architectural styles." },
    { h: "Some of the most common construction methods", list: ["Site Built", "Modular — built in a factory", "Prefabricated (Prefab)", "Manufactured Homes — built on a chassis to federal HUD standards and can be moved", "Panelized Construction", "Structural Insulated Panels (SIPs)", "Timber Frame", "Steel Frame", "Cabin Kits", "A-Frame", "Container Homes"] },
    { p: "Each offers different levels of design flexibility, construction timelines, engineering requirements, price ranges, and installation methods." },
    { p: "The purpose of this chapter is not to recommend one type over another. Instead, it is to introduce you to the many options available so you understand today's ADU marketplace before making a decision." },
    { remember: "There is no single \"best\" ADU. The best ADU is the one that best fits your goals, property, lifestyle, and budget." },
  ],
  m1c5: [
    { p: "Homeowners often use the terms Tiny Home and ADU interchangeably." },
    { p: "Tiny Home generally describes the size of a home. ADU is the term most municipalities use to describe a legal residential dwelling located on the same property as a primary residence." },
    { p: "Throughout this course, we use the term ADU because it is the language used by cities, planning departments, builders, and manufacturers." },
    { p: "When researching local regulations, permits, and planning requirements, always search using the term ADU." },
    { p: "Many homeowners will continue to refer to their project as a Tiny Home, and there is nothing wrong with that. Just remember that your city will most likely refer to it as an ADU." },
    { remember: "Use the term ADU when researching local regulations and communicating with your municipality." },
  ],
  m1c6: [
    { p: "ADUs have become one of the fastest-growing housing trends in the United States. Rising home prices, higher interest rates, evolving family needs, and advancements in construction methods have made ADUs an increasingly attractive option for homeowners." },
    { p: "For many, an ADU provides additional living space for aging parents, adult children, or extended family. Others build ADUs to generate rental income (where permitted), create a private home office, accommodate caregivers or guests, or enhance the overall value and functionality of their property." },
    { p: "At the same time, the ADU industry has evolved significantly. Factory-built construction, modular homes, prefabricated systems, new building materials, and improved engineering have reduced construction timelines while expanding design possibilities. Additionally, artificial intelligence, 3D design software, and digital planning tools are transforming how ADUs are designed, estimated, and constructed." },
    { p: "Today's homeowners are no longer limited to one or two construction methods. Instead, they can evaluate a wide range of products, floor plans, architectural styles, and construction systems before making a decision." },
    { h: "Most people build an ADU for one or more of these reasons", list: ["Housing for aging parents", "Housing for adult children", "Multi-generational living", "Rental income (where permitted)", "Guest accommodations", "Home office or studio", "Caregiver housing", "Flexible living space", "Increasing property value", "Retirement planning"] },
    { p: "Building an ADU is about creating functional, adaptable living space that aligns with your needs while maximizing the potential of your property." },
    { remember: "People build ADUs for many different reasons. The best ADU is one that supports your goals, fits your property, and complies with local regulations." },
  ],
  m1c7: [
    { p: "Many homeowners begin researching ADUs using information gathered from social media, television, friends, or online videos. While some of this information can be helpful, much of it is incomplete or only applicable in specific locations." },
    { p: "Understanding these common misconceptions can help you avoid costly mistakes and unnecessary frustration." },
    { h: "1. Every Tiny Home Is an ADU", p: "Not every Tiny Home qualifies as a legal ADU. An ADU must comply with state and local regulations and is typically built on the same property as a primary residence. While many ADUs are small, the terms are not interchangeable." },
    { h: "2. Every City Has the Same Rules", p: "Each municipality establishes its own zoning requirements within the framework of state law. Setbacks, height limits, lot coverage, parking requirements, utility requirements, and maximum ADU size can vary significantly from one location to another. Never assume your neighbor's property has the same building opportunities as yours." },
    { h: "3. I Should Pick My ADU Before Researching My Property", p: "A more effective approach is to first determine what can legally be built on your property. Once you understand your property's limitations and opportunities, selecting an ADU becomes significantly easier." },
    { h: "4. There Are Only a Few Types of ADUs", p: "Today's ADU market includes more than 10 construction methods and product types, most of which are available in a variety of architectural styles. There are over 30 distinct architectural home styles — so there could be 300+ ADU options available to you. Comparing your options before making a decision can save you both time and money." },
    { h: "5. Builders Will Teach Me Everything I Need to Know", p: "Builders are experts in construction, city officials enforce regulations, and inspectors ensure compliance during construction. However, it is ultimately the homeowner's responsibility to understand the process and make informed decisions before committing to a project. This course and the personalized Property Feasibility Study will teach you the process, introduce you to ADU products, help you create a realistic budget, and prepare you to request builder bids with the right information and questions. That is why we emphasize the framework: Learn → Verify → Review → Plan → Verify → Build." },
    { h: "6. Building an ADU Starts With Getting Prices", p: "While pricing is important, it should not be your first step. Before requesting estimates, verify what can legally be built, determine where the ADU can be placed, understand pre-site costs, and establish a realistic project budget. Better information leads to more accurate pricing." },
    { h: "7. Building an ADU Is Just Buying a Small House", p: "An ADU project involves far more than purchasing a structure. It includes planning, zoning, utilities, permits, site preparation, inspections, construction, and final occupancy. Building an ADU is a process — not simply a purchase." },
    { remember: "Prepared homeowners make better decisions. The more you learn before selecting an ADU or engaging with builders, the more confident and informed your decisions will be throughout the project." },
  ],

  // ── MODULE 2: Understanding City & State ADU Regulations ──
  m2c1: [{"p":"Before choosing an ADU design, talking to builders, or estimating costs, you must first understand the regulations that apply to your property. This is one of the biggest mistakes homeowners make — they begin shopping for an ADU before knowing what they are legally allowed to build."},{"p":"Unlike many home improvement projects, an ADU is regulated by multiple agencies. State laws establish the overall framework, but local governments determine how those laws are applied. In some cases, HOA rules, deed restrictions, or historic district requirements may also affect your project. Understanding these regulations early saves time, prevents costly mistakes, and helps you make informed decisions throughout the planning process."},{"p":"That is why ADUAtlas begins with education. Our goal is not to sell you an ADU — it is to prepare you to build the right ADU for your property, your budget, and your long-term goals. Building an ADU is a process, not just a purchase."},{"h":"The ADUAtlas framework","list":["Learn — understand the ADU process before making decisions","Verify — confirm your local regulations and property requirements","Review — evaluate your options, costs, and risks","Plan — develop a realistic project plan and budget","Verify — confirm your plans before moving forward","Build — move forward with confidence using qualified professionals"]},{"p":"Following this process helps homeowners avoid unnecessary delays, reduce costly mistakes, and increase the likelihood of successfully building a legal ADU. The better prepared you are before contacting a builder, the more informed your decisions will be throughout the entire project."},{"remember":"Understand the regulations that apply to your property before choosing a design or talking to builders. An ADU is regulated by multiple agencies — and education comes first."}],
  m2c2: [{"p":"One of the biggest surprises for homeowners is learning there are no national ADU regulations. While all 50 states can potentially allow Accessory Dwelling Units, the regulations that affect your project are determined primarily by your state and your local city or county. Think of it this way: the State creates the framework for the structure; the City determines what and where it can actually be built."},{"h":"State building codes: the IRC","p":"Most states have adopted the International Residential Code (IRC), which establishes minimum standards for residential construction. Its purpose is to protect public health, safety, and welfare while providing consistent construction standards."},{"h":"The IRC provides consistent requirements for","list":["Structural construction","Fire safety","Plumbing","Electrical systems","Mechanical systems","Energy efficiency"]},{"h":"Local zoning regulations","p":"Zoning is different. Every city has the authority to establish its own development standards, and zoning determines where an ADU can be built and what restrictions apply to your property. As a result, two nearly identical properties in neighboring cities may have very different ADU opportunities."},{"h":"What local zoning typically controls","list":["Maximum ADU size","Minimum lot size","Setbacks","Height limits","Lot coverage","Parking requirements","Utility requirements","Design standards","Permit process and inspections"]},{"remember":"The IRC defines minimum structure requirements; your city determines the ADU building regulations. Building codes are fairly consistent state to state — zoning is local."}],
  m2c3: [{"p":"Understanding the most common regulations will help you better evaluate your property and ask informed questions throughout the planning process. Every item below is set locally — treat each one as a question for your city."},{"h":"The most common city ADU regulations","list":["Zoning eligibility — determines whether detached ADUs are allowed on your property","Minimum lot size — some cities require a minimum lot size before an ADU is permitted","Maximum ADU size — limits the allowable square footage, often between 800 and 1,200 sq ft or a percentage of the primary home","Setbacks — the minimum distance from property lines and existing structures","Height limits — determine whether one- or two-story ADUs are permitted","Lot coverage — limits the percentage of the property that can be covered by structures","Parking requirements — may require additional parking or provide exemptions","Owner occupancy — some jurisdictions require the owner to occupy the primary residence or the ADU","Utility requirements — may require upgrades or additional utility connections","Design standards — some communities regulate roof styles, materials, windows, colors, and exterior appearance","Emergency access — ensures fire and emergency personnel can safely access the ADU","Permit requirements — planning review, building permits, inspections, and final approval","Short-term rental rules — some cities regulate or prohibit rentals of fewer than 31 days","Historic district requirements — additional design review and approvals may be required","HOA or deed restrictions — private restrictions may impose requirements beyond city regulations"]},{"p":"For example, your city may allow an 800-square-foot ADU. But if required setbacks, utility easements, or the location of your existing home leave only a 500-square-foot buildable area, the maximum size allowed by regulation may not be achievable on your property."},{"p":"This is why the ADUAtlas Property Feasibility Study is so valuable: it organizes many of these regulations and applies them to your specific property before you spend thousands of dollars on surveys, engineering, architectural plans, permits, or builder consultations."},{"remember":"A regulation's maximum is not a promise. Setbacks, easements, and your existing home can shrink what actually fits — verify each regulation with your local planning department."}],
  m2c4: [{"p":"Every city has its own ADU regulations, but many requirements fall within similar ranges. The examples below are intended to show what you may encounter. Your city's regulations may be more or less restrictive, so always verify local requirements before making design decisions."},{"h":"Typical ranges you may encounter","list":["Maximum ADU size — 800–1,200 sq ft, or no more than 50% of the primary home's square footage","Side and rear setbacks — typically 4–10 feet from the property line","Separation from the primary home — often 6–10 feet or more, depending on local code and fire requirements","Height limits — typically 16–25 feet","Stories — one story is most common; some cities allow two-story detached ADUs","Parking — you know your street; if parking is a problem, your city knows it too. Verify parking requirements","Minimum lot size — most cities establish one before an ADU is permitted","Utility connections — existing capacity may determine whether upgrades are required","Short-term rentals — many cities prohibit or regulate rentals of fewer than 31 days","Owner occupancy — few jurisdictions require it; most do not"]},{"p":"Remember that these are examples — not guarantees. Two properties with similar dimensions may still have different development opportunities based on easements, topography, utility locations, zoning overlays, environmental restrictions, or other site conditions."},{"remember":"These ranges are examples, not guarantees. Understanding how the regulations apply to your property is what determines what you can build."}],
  m2c5: [{"p":"One of the most common questions homeowners ask is: “Can I build an ADU on my property?” There isn't a simple yes-or-no answer."},{"h":"Common situations that complicate the answer","list":["A relatively small primary home — some cities limit the ADU to a percentage of the primary home's square footage","Utility easements crossing the backyard — easements can restrict where an ADU may be located","Undersized utility service — additional bathrooms or kitchens may require water, sewer, gas, or electrical upgrades","Telephone poles or utility equipment in the way — in some cases they can be relocated, buried, or removed; always ask before assuming they cannot be moved","HOA or deed restrictions — private restrictions may affect your project even when the city allows ADUs","Historic district requirements — additional design review and approvals may be required","Parking requirements — local rules can reduce available building space","Topography or retaining walls — sloping lots often require additional engineering and site preparation"]},{"p":"Two homes on the same street can have completely different ADU opportunities because of lot dimensions, zoning overlays, utility locations, easements, existing home placement, topography, or environmental conditions."},{"p":"This is why ADUAtlas follows a structured planning process. Instead of making assumptions, homeowners first learn the regulations, verify how they apply to their property, review their options, develop a realistic plan, verify that plan, and then move forward with confidence."},{"remember":"“Can I build an ADU?” has no universal answer. Every property is unique — learn, verify, review, plan, verify, then build."}],
  m2c6: [{"p":"Most homeowners underestimate pre-site costs. Before construction begins, there is a planning process that helps you estimate them. Some builders manage the entire process, while others outsource surveys, engineering, permitting, or site planning. Most builders can do everything — but they will charge for it. Depending on the project, feasibility studies alone can cost between $1,000 and $10,000 and may take several weeks to complete. ADUAtlas was created to help homeowners understand what may be possible before making those larger investments."},{"h":"Typical planning timeline","list":["Property research — 1–2 weeks","Feasibility review — 1–3 weeks","Survey (if required) — 2–6 weeks","Design and site planning — 1–3 months","Permit review — 1–6 months","Construction — varies by ADU type"]},{"h":"Typical construction timelines","list":["Prefabricated or modular ADU — 6–10 months","Detached stick-built ADU — 9–15 months","Custom or complex ADU — 12–18+ months"]},{"h":"Typical permit costs","p":"Planning review, building permits, engineering review, utility approvals, impact fees, inspections, corrections, and resubmittals typically range from $2,000 to $15,000 or more, depending on the city and the complexity of the project. Most ADU projects also require multiple inspections throughout construction — site preparation, foundation, framing, utility connections, and final inspection. Once permits are approved, the builder typically manages construction, inspections, and final approvals."},{"p":"The ADUAtlas Property Feasibility Study is not intended to replace your builder, architect, engineer, surveyor, or city planning department. It provides an organized starting point that helps you understand your property before spending significant time and money — and you can refine these estimates using the interactive planning worksheets included with your study."},{"remember":"Good planning reduces surprises. Pre-site planning has real costs and timelines — permits alone typically run $2,000–$15,000 or more and months of review, and all of it varies by city."}],
  m2c7: [{"p":"California has become one of the nation's leaders in ADU development. In an effort to increase housing opportunities, the state has passed legislation that encourages cities to allow Accessory Dwelling Units while still permitting local governments to regulate how those ADUs are developed. As a result, many California cities have created detailed ADU webpages, downloadable guides, interactive zoning maps, permit checklists, and homeowner resources designed to simplify the process. Although every city is different, the overall process is often similar."},{"h":"A typical city ADU process","list":["Confirm your property's zoning designation","Review local ADU regulations","Verify setback, height, and lot coverage requirements","Determine utility requirements","Review parking requirements and exemptions","Decide which type of ADU best fits your property","Prepare preliminary plans","Submit permit applications","Complete required plan reviews","Obtain permits and begin construction","Complete required inspections","Receive final approval or Certificate of Occupancy"]},{"p":"You can often access your lot dimensions and zoning through your city's property records portal using an address lookup tool. While these resources are extremely helpful, they are often spread across multiple departments — Planning, Building, Public Works, Engineering, GIS Mapping, Utilities, Fire, and Assessor records. The information exists, but it is rarely organized from the homeowner's perspective. That is one reason homeowners often become overwhelmed — and why ADUAtlas organizes this information into a structured planning process."},{"remember":"The information homeowners need usually exists on city websites — just spread across many departments. Regardless of where you live, always rely on your local building department as the final authority for regulations affecting your property."}],
  m2c8: [{"p":"One of the biggest challenges homeowners face is determining what may actually be possible on their property before spending thousands of dollars on surveys, architectural plans, engineering, or builder consultations. The ADUAtlas Property Feasibility Study was created to bridge that gap. Rather than beginning with a tape measure, the study organizes available property information and combines it with applicable ADU regulations to help you better understand your options."},{"h":"Questions the study helps answer","list":["Is my property likely to qualify for a detached ADU?","Approximately how much buildable area may be available?","Which ADU sizes may fit?","Which regulations are most likely to affect my project?","What potential challenges should I investigate before moving forward?","What questions should I ask my city and my builder?"]},{"p":"The Property Feasibility Study is intended to educate and prepare homeowners. It is not a substitute for the surveys, engineering, or architectural plans some cities require. Combined with the ADUAtlas course, worksheets, builder-preparation documents, and planning tools, it helps homeowners move from guessing to planning."},{"h":"The goal is simple","list":["Educate first","Verify your property","Review your options","Develop a realistic plan","Then move forward with confidence"]},{"remember":"The Feasibility Study organizes property information and applicable regulations so you move from guessing to planning. It educates and prepares — it does not replace surveys, engineering, or your city."}],
  m2c9: [{"p":"Understanding ADU regulations is one of the most important steps in the entire ADU process. Although building codes establish minimum construction standards, local zoning regulations determine what can legally be built on your property."},{"p":"Every property is unique. Two homes on the same street may have completely different ADU opportunities because of lot dimensions, the primary home's square footage, easements, utility locations, zoning overlays, or topography. The ADUAtlas planning process prepares homeowners to ask better questions, make informed decisions, and understand their options before moving forward."},{"h":"By completing this module, you should now understand","list":["The difference between building codes and zoning regulations","The most common regulations that affect detached ADUs","Why every property should be evaluated individually","Why regulations must be verified before selecting an ADU","How the ADUAtlas Property Feasibility Study helps organize information before major expenses are incurred"]},{"remember":"The more prepared you are today, the more confident your decisions will be tomorrow."}],
  // ── MODULE 3: The 10-Step ADU Process ──
  m3c1: [{"p":"Before spending significant money on surveys, engineering, architectural plans, or permits, take time to understand the entire ADU process. The more informed you become, the better decisions you will make throughout your project."},{"h":"Understand and verify the following to start","list":["ADU regulations","Budgeting","Timelines","ADU types","Common mistakes","Builder expectations"]},{"h":"What to watch for","p":"Review all pre-site requirements along with your city's construction permit application requirements and checklists. Many homeowners become attached to an ADU design before understanding what is allowed on their property — an entire module of this course (False Starts) is devoted to catching that early."},{"remember":"Education comes before money. The more informed you are, the better every later decision becomes."}],
  m3c2: [{"p":"The ADUAtlas Property Feasibility Study helps determine what may realistically fit on your property at a fraction of what a builder or a survey company would charge. Before deciding what you would like to build, determine what you can legally build."},{"h":"The report should include","list":["Parcel dimensions and any restrictions, such as obstacles or topography","Primary structure details","Local ADU regulations","Buildable area","Utility considerations","Preliminary budget guidance"]},{"h":"What to watch for","p":"The Feasibility Study should identify major challenges or false starts before major money is spent."},{"remember":"Before deciding what you'd like to build, determine what you can legally build."}],
  m3c3: [{"p":"Regulations vary by city, ZIP code, and sometimes even by address. Verify: everything."},{"h":"Verify at minimum","list":["ADU size limits","Setbacks","Height restrictions","Parking requirements","Occupancy requirements","Utility requirements"]},{"h":"What to watch for","p":"Always verify the regulations until you have a building permit — cities sometimes change regulations without much notice. Verify the regulations included in your Feasibility Study, too. And don't forget your HOA."},{"remember":"Keep verifying until you hold a building permit. Regulations vary by city, ZIP code, and sometimes by address — and they can change."}],
  m3c4: [{"p":"Establish a realistic budget before anything else. Why move forward without a good estimate?"},{"h":"Potential costs include","list":["Site preparation","Utility connections","Permits","Engineering","Survey","Foundation","Landscaping","The ADU structure and associated expenses"]},{"h":"What to watch for","p":"Everything mentioned in the course — there are a lot of details to be calculated. The budget worksheets walk you through them."},{"remember":"A realistic budget covers far more than the structure. Estimate before you commit."}],
  m3c5: [{"p":"Explore all the ADU options available in the ADU Universe module. There are charts showing advantages, price differences, and styles — with over 1,000 possible combinations."},{"h":"Construction types include, but are not limited to","list":["Manufactured","Modular","Stick-built","Kits","Containers","3D printed","And more"]},{"h":"What to watch for","p":"The least expensive ADU is not always the best deal — or even legal. Investigate!"},{"remember":"The least expensive ADU is not always the best deal — or even legal for your lot. Investigate before you commit."}],
  m3c6: [{"p":"Some cities require an official survey before issuing permits, and some do not. You can often verify property details on your city's website in the real-estate tax records section."},{"h":"A survey will verify","list":["Property boundaries","Existing structures","Easements","Utility locations"]},{"h":"What to watch for","p":"Survey requirements and survey companies are very different. You probably don't need air or mineral rights determined — if a survey is required, establish exactly what type you need, because there are many available."},{"remember":"If your city requires a survey, find out which type — survey requirements and survey companies differ widely."}],
  m3c7: [{"p":"Once you have selected an ADU type and established a budget, a Site Plan may be required — and it is a good place to plan everything besides the structure: a patio, a garden, parking, landscaping. The Feasibility Study is a preliminary plan that establishes legal boundaries with a reasonable pre-site estimate; the Site Plan is where you design the whole space around it. Some cities require a Site Plan, and some elements of yours may not require permits."},{"h":"A typical Site Plan shows","list":["Property lines — is there extra space for a garden?","The primary residence — maybe a stone walkway between the two buildings","Proposed ADU location","Required setbacks","Driveways and access","Above-ground and underground utility connections","Landscaping"]},{"h":"What to watch for","p":"This is where you create the entire space — a garden hose, a fire pit, parking, a patio, and everything in addition to the regulations. Verify regulations and walk the site with your builder before construction begins."},{"remember":"A Feasibility Study determines what may legally fit; a Site Plan lays out the space you'll actually live with. Walk the site with your builder before construction begins."}],
  m3c8: [{"p":"Use your Property Feasibility Study and project goals to compare builders. Most builders specialize in a particular type of construction, and they advertise that. Choose a builder familiar with the construction type and style you want — a stick-built contractor can manage the assembly of many building types, but a modular company cannot build a stick-built home. Tell them or show them what you want, and ask if they have experience building the type of ADU you want. The Feasibility Study includes a worksheet with questions to ask every builder, which helps both you and the builder determine fit."},{"h":"Review","list":["Experience","Portfolio","References","Warranty","Construction method"]},{"h":"What to watch for","list":["Builders who do not provide itemized proposals or even a ballpark timeline","Bids that exclude pre-site work, such as foundations or utility connections"]},{"p":"Experienced rehabbers often say projects cost twice as much and take twice as long as originally expected. New construction typically has fewer surprises, but with so many variables involved, always prepare for schedule adjustments and cost changes."},{"remember":"Compare builders on experience, portfolio, references, warranty, and construction method — and be wary of bids that aren't itemized or exclude pre-site work."}],
  m3c9: [{"p":"This step is sometimes referred to as the Land Development Manual or Project Submittal Process. Builders, architects, and designers often assist with permit applications. Review your city's website for its permit application and fee schedule."},{"h":"Permits commonly require","list":["Site Plan","Construction plans","Structural review","Utility permits","Final inspection","Occupancy approval"]},{"h":"What to watch for","p":"Permit requirements, timelines, and fees vary significantly by city. Before you begin, review your local building department's requirements — many jurisdictions provide online permit application portals and submission checklists."},{"remember":"Permit requirements, timelines, and fees vary significantly by city — your building department's checklist is the roadmap."}],
  m3c10: [{"p":"Once permits are approved, construction can begin."},{"h":"Typical phases include","list":["Site preparation","Foundation","Utility installation","Structure installation","Interior and exterior finishes","Final inspections"]},{"p":"Upon approval, the city may issue a Certificate of Occupancy, or equivalent authorization, allowing the ADU to be occupied."},{"h":"What to watch for","p":"Unexpected site conditions and utility issues remain among the most common causes of project delays."},{"h":"Key takeaway","p":"Building an ADU is a process, not a single decision. Following these ten steps before you build will help you understand your property, establish realistic expectations, compare builders more effectively, and reduce costly surprises before construction begins."},{"remember":"The more prepared you are before breaking ground, the more likely your project stays on budget, remains on schedule, and achieves the results you expect."}],
  // ── MODULE 4: The ADU Universe — 25+ Types & Construction Methods ──
  m4c1: [{"p": "Have you ever noticed that no two homes look exactly alike? Some are modern with clean lines and large windows. Others have the charm of a cottage, the warmth of a cabin, or the timeless look of a farmhouse. Some are built entirely on the property, while others arrive from a factory almost complete."}, {"p": "If you've spent any time researching ADUs online, you've probably realized there are an incredible number of choices. In fact, many homeowners become overwhelmed before they ever speak with a builder. The good news is that you don't need to become an architect, builder, or engineer to understand your options. You simply need a framework — and that's exactly what this module provides."}, {"p": "Throughout this chapter, we'll organize what ADUAtlas calls the ADU Universe into a simple system that's easy to understand. Instead of looking at hundreds of homes individually, you'll learn how to group them into categories so you can compare similar options and eliminate those that don't fit your property, budget, or goals. Think of this chapter as the roadmap for everything that follows."}, {"h": "Why Are There So Many Choices?", "p": "Twenty years ago, most homeowners had only one practical option — hire a contractor and build an ADU on-site. Today, advances in engineering, manufacturing, technology, transportation, and construction have changed everything. Entire sections of homes can be built inside climate-controlled factories, wall systems can be manufactured with incredible precision, and floor plans can be customized using advanced design software. Manufacturers now offer hundreds of models in a wide variety of sizes, layouts, finishes, and architectural styles. While having more options is exciting, it creates one important challenge: how do you compare them?"}, {"h": "The ADUAtlas Approach", "p": "Before selecting an ADU, we believe homeowners should first understand what they're comparing. That means learning the difference between construction methods, architectural styles, floor plans, building systems, pricing, and intended use. Once you understand those categories, comparing ADUs becomes much easier. Instead of guessing, you'll know which questions to ask and which options deserve further consideration."}, {"remember": "The ADU marketplace has grown tremendously over the past decade. Rather than trying to compare hundreds of individual homes, begin by understanding the major categories that make up the ADU Universe. Once you understand the framework, selecting the right ADU becomes much easier."}],
  m4c2: [{"p": "One of the biggest surprises homeowners have is believing they are choosing from twenty or thirty different types of ADUs. In reality, you're choosing from hundreds of possible combinations. Why? Because every ADU is made up of several different decisions — how it's built, its architectural style, its size, floor plan, exterior finishes, roofing, windows, and intended use. Every one of those decisions creates another combination. That's why two homes may look almost identical but be built completely differently, and why two modular homes may have completely different styles and floor plans."}, {"p": "The important thing to remember is that not every term describes the same thing. Some describe how the ADU is built. Others describe what it looks like. Others describe how you'll use it. Throughout this module, we'll organize the ADU Universe into three simple layers."}, {"h": "Layer One — Construction Method", "p": "This answers one question: how is the ADU built? Examples include site-built, modular, manufactured, panelized, structural insulated panels (SIPs), steel frame, timber frame, container construction, and kit construction. These methods affect construction schedules, engineering, transportation, installation, inspections, and overall project cost."}, {"h": "Layer Two — Architectural Style", "p": "This answers a different question: what do you want your ADU to look like? Examples include modern, traditional, farmhouse, cottage, cabin, contemporary, luxury, A-frame, and craftsman. The architectural style creates the personality of the home, but it does not determine how it's built. A modern ADU may be site-built or modular; a farmhouse may be panelized or custom-built; a cabin may arrive as a kit or be constructed entirely on-site. Style and construction method are two different decisions."}, {"h": "Layer Three — Size, Floor Plan & Intended Use", "p": "How will you use your ADU, today or ten years from now? Will it be a rental, a home for aging parents, a guest house, a home office, housing for adult children, or a future retirement home? Those answers influence the size, number of bedrooms and bathrooms, storage, accessibility, and overall floor plan. The intended use often determines the design — which is why there are literally hundreds of possible ADU combinations."}, {"remember": "Every ADU is a combination of three primary decisions — how it is built, what it looks like, and how it will be used. Understanding these three layers gives you a simple framework for comparing hundreds of possible ADU options without becoming overwhelmed."}],
  m4c3: [{"p": "For decades, site-built construction has been the traditional way to build a home. The process is familiar: materials are delivered to your property, and the home is built one step at a time by skilled tradespeople. The foundation is poured, the walls are framed, the roof is installed, utilities are connected, and the interior and exterior are completed on-site. Most people picture this type of construction when they think about building a home."}, {"p": "A custom site-built ADU offers the greatest design flexibility because it is designed specifically for your property. Every detail can be customized, from the floor plan and window placement to the roofline, exterior finishes, and architectural style. If your property has an unusual shape, significant slope, mature trees, difficult access, or unique design requirements, a custom-built ADU may provide solutions that factory-built homes cannot. Because the home is constructed on-site, builders can make adjustments during construction if necessary."}, {"p": "The tradeoff is time. Weather, inspections, material deliveries, subcontractor schedules, and labor availability can all affect the construction schedule. Depending on the project, site-built ADUs often require more time to complete than some factory-built alternatives. Another advantage of custom construction is architectural compatibility: if your goal is to make the ADU look like it was built at the same time as the primary residence, a custom design often provides the greatest opportunity to match rooflines, siding, trim, windows, and other details."}, {"h": "Advantages", "list": ["Maximum design flexibility", "Custom floor plans", "Easier to match the existing home", "Well suited for challenging properties", "Wide selection of builders and materials"]}, {"h": "Considerations", "list": ["Longer construction schedules", "More on-site activity", "Weather can affect progress", "Construction costs may vary as the project develops"]}, {"h": "Best For", "list": ["Homeowners who want a highly customized ADU designed specifically for their property and are willing to trade a longer construction schedule for greater flexibility"]}, {"remember": "A custom site-built ADU offers the greatest design flexibility and can be tailored to almost any property. While construction may take longer, it allows homeowners to create an ADU that complements their existing home, fits their property, and supports their long-term goals."}],
  m4c4: [{"p": "One of the fastest-growing segments of the ADU industry is factory-built construction. Instead of building every part of the home on your property, many manufacturers construct all or part of the ADU inside a controlled factory environment before delivering it to the building site. This approach can improve quality control because construction takes place indoors where weather has little impact on the process. Factory production also allows manufacturers to repeat proven construction methods, reduce material waste, and streamline production."}, {"p": "Factory-built does not describe one specific type of ADU. It is a broad category that includes several different construction methods, including modular homes, manufactured homes, panelized systems, and other prefabricated components. That distinction is important. Many homeowners use the terms prefab, modular, and manufactured interchangeably, but they are not the same. Over the next several chapters, we'll explain each construction method separately so you understand the differences."}, {"p": "Although much of the construction may take place in a factory, every factory-built ADU still requires work on the property. Depending on the manufacturer and product, your project may still require site preparation, foundation construction, utility connections, transportation, crane placement, assembly, interior and exterior finish work, final inspections, and landscaping and site restoration. For this reason, it is important to understand exactly what is included in a manufacturer's quoted price. A factory-built ADU may be advertised at an attractive price, but that price may represent only one portion of the completed project."}, {"h": "Advantages", "list": ["Factory-controlled construction environment", "Consistent manufacturing process", "Reduced weather delays during production", "Potentially shorter overall construction schedules", "Less on-site disruption during portions of construction"]}, {"h": "Considerations", "list": ["Transportation requirements", "Property access for delivery", "Foundation and site work still required", "Installation requirements vary by manufacturer", "Quoted prices may not include the complete project"]}, {"h": "Best For", "list": ["Homeowners who want to explore alternatives to traditional site-built construction and are interested in understanding how factory-built homes are designed, transported, and completed"]}, {"remember": "Factory-built ADUs represent one of the fastest-growing areas of residential construction. While much of the home may be built in a factory, every project still requires planning, site preparation, installation, and local construction. Understanding exactly what is included in the quoted price is essential before comparing different manufacturers or products."}],
  m4c5: [{"p": "Not every ADU is built one board at a time. Some homes are constructed using engineered building systems that improve efficiency, reduce construction time, and provide consistent quality. Instead of assembling every wall and roof from individual pieces of lumber, many manufacturers produce large sections of the home in a factory under controlled conditions. Two of the most common engineered systems are Panelized Construction and Structural Insulated Panels (SIPs). Although they're often grouped together, they are not the same."}, {"h": "Panelized Construction", "p": "Panelized construction uses factory-built wall, floor, and roof sections that are transported to the building site and assembled on the foundation. Think of it as assembling a large puzzle: instead of thousands of individual pieces of lumber, the builder receives large, pre-built sections that fit together according to the engineered plans. Once the panels are assembled, local contractors complete the roofing, utilities, insulation (if required), drywall, and finishes. Because much of the framing is already done, panelized homes may reduce framing time while still allowing flexibility in the final design."}, {"h": "Panelized — Advantages", "list": ["Faster framing than traditional construction", "Factory precision", "Reduced material waste", "Less weather exposure during manufacturing", "Flexible floor plans"]}, {"h": "Panelized — Considerations", "list": ["Local assembly still required", "Weather can affect on-site construction", "Delivery access is still important", "Construction schedules vary by manufacturer"]}, {"h": "Structural Insulated Panels (SIPs)", "p": "SIPs are a high-performance building system. Instead of framing a wall with individual studs and then adding insulation later, each SIP is manufactured as one engineered panel consisting of rigid insulation sandwiched between structural facings. The result is a strong, energy-efficient wall system that can often be assembled more quickly than conventional framing. Many homeowners choose SIPs for their excellent thermal performance and airtight building envelope. However, SIPs require proper engineering, careful installation, and experienced contractors who understand the system."}, {"h": "SIPs — Advantages", "list": ["Excellent energy efficiency", "Strong structural performance", "Faster enclosure of the building", "Reduced air infiltration", "Consistent factory manufacturing"]}, {"h": "SIPs — Considerations", "list": ["Requires experienced installers", "Design changes are more difficult after manufacturing", "Engineering is critical", "Material costs may differ from conventional framing"]}, {"h": "Panelized vs. SIP", "p": "Panelized construction generally refers to framed wall, floor, and roof sections assembled on-site. SIPs are engineered structural panels that combine framing and insulation into one building component. Neither system is automatically better than the other. The best choice depends on your project, budget, energy goals, local builder experience, and manufacturer."}, {"remember": "Panelized construction and SIPs are two different engineered building systems. Both can improve efficiency and quality, but each has unique advantages and considerations. Understanding how these systems work will help you compare builders and manufacturers more confidently."}],
  m4c6: [{"p": "One of the fastest-growing segments of the small-home industry is the kit home. Kit homes have been around for more than a century, but today's versions are very different from those sold decades ago. Modern kit homes range from simple structural packages to highly engineered building systems that include pre-cut materials, detailed construction plans, hardware, and — in some cases — windows, doors, roofing materials, and exterior finishes. Some companies also offer cabin packages or shell packages that let homeowners complete portions of the project themselves or hire local contractors."}, {"p": "Because the level of completion varies from one manufacturer to another, it is important to understand exactly what is included before comparing prices. One kit may include only the structural materials. Another may include windows, doors, roofing, siding, and detailed construction drawings. Others may provide nearly everything except the foundation, utilities, interior finishes, and local labor. For homeowners who enjoy being involved in the construction process, kit homes may be an attractive alternative. However, assembling a kit home still requires planning, permits, inspections, skilled labor, and coordination between multiple contractors. The kit is only one part of the completed project."}, {"h": "Advantages", "list": ["Wide variety of architectural styles", "Flexible price points", "Factory precision", "Opportunity for owner participation", "Available from many manufacturers"]}, {"h": "Considerations", "list": ["Contents vary significantly between manufacturers", "Local labor is usually required", "Foundation and utilities are separate costs", "Permits and inspections still apply", "Delivery and storage should be planned before materials arrive"]}, {"h": "Questions to Ask Before Purchasing a Kit Home", "list": ["What exactly is included?", "What is not included?", "Who prepares the foundation?", "Who installs the kit?", "Are engineering plans included?", "What permits are required?", "What local work will still need to be completed?", "What warranties are provided?"]}, {"remember": "Kit homes and cabin packages can be an excellent option for some homeowners, but every manufacturer offers a different level of completion. Before comparing prices, make sure you understand exactly what the package includes and what work will still need to be completed on your property."}],
  m4c7: [{"p": "The popularity of tiny homes has helped introduce millions of people to the idea of living in a smaller, more efficient space. Television shows, social media, and innovative designs have made tiny living more appealing than ever. Although the terms tiny home and ADU are often used interchangeably, they are not always the same. A tiny home generally describes the size of a dwelling; an ADU is defined by state and local regulations. Whether a tiny home qualifies as a legal ADU depends on where it is built and whether it complies with local building and zoning requirements. Some tiny homes are built on permanent foundations, while others are constructed on trailers. In many jurisdictions, a tiny home on a permanent foundation may qualify as an ADU if it meets all applicable codes and regulations."}, {"p": "The appeal of tiny living goes beyond square footage. Many homeowners appreciate the efficient use of space, lower maintenance requirements, and thoughtful design that lets every square foot serve a purpose. Today's tiny homes often include full kitchens, bathrooms, living areas, one or more sleeping spaces, built-in storage, and outdoor living areas. The challenge is balancing comfort with available space. A well-designed 500-square-foot home can often feel much larger than a poorly designed 700-square-foot home — the floor plan frequently matters more than the total square footage."}, {"h": "Park Models", "p": "Park models are another small-home option some homeowners consider. These homes are designed to maximize living space within a compact footprint and are often found in recreational or planned communities. Whether a park model may be used as an ADU depends entirely on state and local regulations. Always verify local requirements before purchasing one for use as an ADU."}, {"h": "Backyard Pods & Bunkies", "p": "Backyard pods and bunkies have become increasingly popular for home offices, guest rooms, exercise studios, hobby spaces, and creative work areas. Some include plumbing and kitchens, while others are designed as accessory structures without full living facilities. If you intend to use a pod or bunkie as an ADU, verify that it complies with your local building codes and zoning requirements."}, {"h": "Advantages", "list": ["Efficient use of space", "Lower maintenance", "Flexible designs", "Attractive for smaller lots", "Often faster to complete than larger homes"]}, {"h": "Considerations", "list": ["Limited storage", "Smaller living areas", "Local regulations vary", "Not every tiny home qualifies as an ADU"]}, {"remember": "Tiny homes, park models, backyard pods, and bunkies all offer creative small-living solutions, but they are not automatically legal ADUs. Before purchasing any of these options, verify that they comply with your local regulations and support your intended use."}],
  m4c8: [{"p": "Innovation continues to reshape the housing industry, and today's homeowners have access to construction methods rarely considered just a few years ago. Although these options represent a small portion of the overall ADU market, they continue to gain attention because of their unique appearance, construction methods, and potential advantages. Like every ADU, these homes should be evaluated based on your property, local regulations, builder experience, budget, and intended use."}, {"h": "Container Homes", "p": "Container homes have become one of the most recognizable alternative housing options. Builders modify retired or purpose-built steel shipping containers into living spaces by adding insulation, windows, doors, utilities, and interior finishes. Some homeowners appreciate their contemporary appearance and modular nature; others are drawn to combining multiple containers into larger floor plans. Construction requires careful engineering, insulation, moisture control, and structural modifications. The finished home often looks very different from the original shipping container."}, {"h": "Container Hybrid Homes", "p": "Container hybrid homes combine one or more shipping containers with conventional wood or steel-framed construction. This allows builders to incorporate the strength and appearance of containers while providing greater flexibility for room sizes, roof designs, and floor plans. Hybrid construction has become increasingly popular because it combines creative design with more traditional building techniques."}, {"h": "Dome & Geodesic Homes", "p": "Dome and geodesic homes use curved structural designs that distribute loads efficiently while creating distinctive architectural spaces. Although visually unique, dome homes require specialized engineering and construction techniques."}, {"h": "Quonset Structures", "p": "Quonset structures use curved steel arches to create durable, open interior spaces. Originally developed for military use, modern Quonset buildings are now used for workshops, garages, agricultural buildings, and in some cases residential construction. Their appearance makes them a distinctive choice for homeowners seeking something different."}, {"h": "3D-Printed Construction", "p": "3D-printed construction is one of the newest developments in residential construction. Large automated printers place specially designed concrete mixtures layer by layer to create portions of a building's structural walls. While this technology continues to evolve, it demonstrates how innovation may influence residential construction in the future. As adoption increases, homeowners can expect to see additional products, manufacturers, and construction methods enter the marketplace."}, {"h": "Advantages", "list": ["Unique architectural designs", "Innovative construction methods", "Growing number of manufacturers", "Opportunity for creative floor plans"]}, {"h": "Considerations", "list": ["Limited builder availability in some areas", "Specialized engineering", "Local regulations vary", "Construction experience may be limited depending on location"]}, {"remember": "Alternative construction methods continue to expand the ADU marketplace. While these homes offer unique design opportunities, every project should still be evaluated based on local regulations, qualified builders, engineering requirements, total project cost, and compatibility with your property."}],
  m4c9: [{"p": "Once you've decided how your ADU will be built, the next decision is what you want it to look like. This is where many homeowners begin their search — saving photographs, creating idea boards, browsing builder websites, and collecting ideas that reflect their personal style. Architectural style is the personality of the home. It influences curb appeal, first impressions, and how well the ADU complements the primary residence. And remember: architectural style and construction method are two different decisions. A modern ADU can be site-built, modular, or panelized. A farmhouse can be custom-built or constructed from a kit. The style you choose does not determine how the home is built."}, {"h": "Popular Architectural Styles", "list": ["Modern — clean lines, simple roof designs, large windows, open interiors, and minimal exterior detailing", "Traditional — familiar rooflines, balanced proportions, shutters, and timeless exterior materials that blend with established neighborhoods", "Farmhouse-inspired — simple forms with welcoming front porches, board-and-batten siding, gable roofs, and warm finishes", "Cottage-style — compact floor plans, charming windows, inviting entries, and traditional detailing for a cozy appearance", "Cabin-inspired — exposed wood, natural materials, covered porches, and a warm rustic appearance", "Luxury Contemporary — premium materials, expansive glass, high ceilings, indoor-outdoor living, and sophisticated architectural details"]}, {"h": "Choosing an Architectural Style", "p": "Many homeowners choose a style they love, but there are other factors worth considering. Ask yourself: Will it complement the existing home? Does it fit the neighborhood? Will the roof design work with local weather? Does the exterior require more maintenance? Will the style still appeal to you years from now? The goal is to create an ADU that looks like it belongs on the property."}, {"remember": "Architectural style determines the appearance and personality of your ADU — not how it is built. Choose a style that complements your property, supports your goals, and creates a home you'll enjoy for years to come."}],
  m4c10: [{"p": "After choosing a construction method and architectural style, the next step is deciding how your ADU will function. This begins with the floor plan. A thoughtful floor plan can make a smaller home feel comfortable, efficient, and surprisingly spacious. In many cases, a well-designed 600-square-foot ADU will live better than a poorly designed home that is much larger. The best floor plan is one that supports how the ADU will be used."}, {"h": "Common Layouts", "list": ["Studio — combines living, sleeping, and kitchen areas into one open space; maximizes efficiency and is often selected for guest houses, rentals, or home offices", "One-bedroom — one of the most popular choices, providing greater privacy while maintaining a relatively compact footprint", "Two-bedroom — may be attractive for small families, caregivers, or long-term rentals where local regulations permit", "Accessible — for aging parents or long-term accessibility, may favor wider hallways, larger bathrooms, fewer steps, and layouts that support mobility"]}, {"h": "Intended Use", "p": "Before selecting a floor plan, ask yourself one simple question: how will this ADU be used? Your answer should guide every design decision. Common uses include long-term rental, family member, aging parents, adult children, guest house, home office, art studio, caregiver housing, and future retirement home. When the intended use is clear, choosing the right layout becomes much easier."}, {"remember": "A successful ADU begins with a floor plan that supports its intended use. Before choosing a design, think about how the space will be used every day. A well-designed layout often adds more value than additional square footage."}],
  m4c11: [{"p": "By now you've explored many of the most common ADU construction methods, architectural styles, and floor plan options. You may already have a few favorites. Now it's time to compare them — and this is where many homeowners make one of the biggest mistakes in the planning process. They compare one feature, usually price, without considering the entire project. The best ADU is not necessarily the least expensive, the largest, or the one with the most attractive photograph. The best ADU is the one that best fits your property, local regulations, budget, lifestyle, and long-term goals. Instead of asking 'Which ADU is best?', ask 'Which ADU is best for my property?'"}, {"h": "Questions to Compare Every ADU", "list": ["Will it fit on my property? Verify it can legally fit within your property's setbacks, height limits, separation requirements, and other local regulations before falling in love with a design.", "Does it fit my budget? Look beyond the advertised price — consider foundation, site preparation, utilities, delivery, crane service, installation, permits, landscaping, and interior upgrades. The completed project cost matters far more than the advertised starting price.", "Does it support my intended use? A guest house may need a different layout than a long-term rental; a home for aging parents may benefit from wider hallways, fewer steps, and an accessible bathroom.", "Can it be delivered or built on my property? Some factory-built homes require wide streets, crane access, or additional staging space; a site-built ADU may be better for properties with difficult access.", "Who will build it? Some builders specialize in site-built homes; others focus on modular, panelized, or container construction. Choose professionals experienced in the type of ADU you're considering."]}, {"h": "Comparison Checklist", "list": ["Construction method", "Architectural style", "Floor plan", "Size", "Energy efficiency", "Foundation requirements", "Construction schedule", "Total project cost", "Builder experience", "Warranty", "Maintenance", "Long-term value"]}, {"p": "Comparing ADUs using the same checklist makes it much easier to evaluate your options fairly."}, {"remember": "Don't compare ADUs based on one feature alone. Compare the complete project, including construction method, total cost, intended use, builder experience, and compatibility with your property. A thoughtful comparison leads to better decisions."}],
  m4c12: [{"p": "One of the most confusing parts of shopping for an ADU is understanding the price. You may see one company advertising a beautiful ADU for $95,000 and another offering a similar-looking home for $165,000. Does that mean one company is dramatically less expensive? Not necessarily. The first question should always be: 'What does the price include?' Different manufacturers include different levels of completion. Some prices cover only the structure. Others include delivery. Some include installation. Others provide a completely finished turnkey project. Unless you're comparing the same scope of work, you're not making an accurate comparison."}, {"h": "Common Pricing Terms", "list": ["Base Price — the starting price for the basic model; often excludes many costs required to complete the project", "Shell Package — generally includes the structural exterior of the building but leaves much of the interior unfinished", "Delivered Price — usually includes manufacturing and transportation to your property; site work and installation may still be additional", "Installed Price — typically includes delivery and installation of the structure; foundation, utilities, permits, landscaping, and finish work may still be separate", "Turnkey Price — generally the closest estimate to a completed ADU ready for occupancy; even then, verify exactly what is included"]}, {"h": "Questions Every Homeowner Should Ask", "list": ["Does this include the foundation?", "Are permits included?", "Is engineering included?", "Are utility connections included?", "Is delivery included?", "Is crane service included?", "Are appliances included?", "What upgrades cost extra?", "What work is my responsibility?"]}, {"h": "Price vs. Value", "p": "The lowest price is not always the best value. Quality construction, experienced builders, better materials, energy efficiency, warranties, and thoughtful design may provide greater long-term value than simply choosing the lowest initial cost. An ADU is a long-term investment; it deserves careful evaluation. As carpenters often say about their work: Good, Cheap, Fast — pick two."}, {"remember": "Before comparing prices, make sure you're comparing the same scope of work. A lower advertised price may not include the foundation, utilities, permits, installation, or many other costs required to complete the project. Understanding what is included is one of the best ways to avoid unexpected expenses."}],
  m4c13: [{"p": "By this point in the course, you've explored many of the most common construction methods, architectural styles, floor plans, and design options available today. You may even have a few favorites. Now it's time to narrow your choices. One of the biggest mistakes homeowners make is selecting an ADU before determining whether it fits their property, budget, and long-term goals. An attractive style or a low advertised price doesn't automatically make it the right choice. The goal is to find the best ADU for your property."}, {"h": "Start with Your Property", "p": "Every property is unique. Before selecting an ADU, consider available building area, setbacks, lot dimensions, utility locations, easements, trees, slope, driveway access, delivery access, and existing structures. A beautiful ADU that cannot legally or practically be built on your property is not the right choice."}, {"h": "Think About the Future", "p": "Many homeowners focus only on today's needs. Instead, consider five, ten, or twenty years ahead. Will the ADU be used for rental income, aging parents, adult children, guests, a caregiver, a home office, or your future retirement? Choosing the right ADU today may eliminate expensive remodeling later."}, {"h": "Balance Wants with Needs", "p": "Every homeowner has a wish list. Separate the features you would like to have from those you truly need. Needs may include one bedroom, an accessible bathroom, a full kitchen, laundry, and storage. Wants may include vaulted ceilings, a covered porch, luxury finishes, an outdoor kitchen, and premium appliances. Understanding the difference helps you stay within your budget without sacrificing functionality."}, {"h": "Compare Before You Commit", "p": "Before making a final decision, compare several options. Review construction methods, floor plan, architectural style, energy efficiency, maintenance, timeline, builder experience, warranty, and total installed cost. Taking the time to compare several options often leads to better long-term decisions."}, {"remember": "The best ADU is not necessarily the largest, least expensive, or most attractive. It is the one that fits your property, supports your intended use, stays within your budget, and helps you achieve your long-term goals."}],
  m4c14: [{"p": "You now have a much better understanding of today's ADU marketplace. You know there are many different construction methods, architectural styles, floor plans, and pricing structures. More importantly, you understand how to compare them. That knowledge puts you in a much stronger position than the average homeowner. Before contacting a builder, take a few minutes to review what you've learned."}, {"h": "Can You Answer These Questions?", "list": ["Which construction methods interest you?", "Which architectural styles do you prefer?", "Approximately how large should your ADU be?", "How will it be used?", "What is your estimated budget?", "What features are most important?", "Which options no longer fit your goals?"]}, {"p": "The more prepared you are, the more productive your conversations with builders will be. Builders appreciate homeowners who understand their objectives and can clearly communicate what they're trying to accomplish. That preparation often results in more accurate proposals, fewer surprises, and better overall decisions."}, {"h": "The ADUAtlas Difference", "p": "Builders build. City officials enforce regulations. Inspectors inspect. ADUAtlas prepares you. Our mission is to help you become an educated homeowner before you invest significant time and money. When you understand the process, you'll ask better questions, compare your options more effectively, and make decisions with greater confidence."}, {"remember": "Preparation is one of the best investments you can make before beginning an ADU project. The more informed you are before meeting with builders, the better your decisions are likely to be throughout the entire process."}],
  // ── MODULE 5: Module 5: Pre-Site Preparation, Budgets & Timelines ──
  m5c1: [{"p": "One of the first questions homeowners ask is, \"How much does an ADU cost?\" The better question is, \"How much will my entire project cost?\""}, {"p": "The ADU structure is only one part of the investment. Before construction begins, you should understand the work required to prepare your property. Utility connections, permits, inspections, and site preparation can significantly affect your total budget."}, {"h": "What planning ahead does for you", "p": "Planning before construction begins helps you create a realistic budget, compare builder proposals more accurately, and reduce costly surprises. No two properties are exactly alike—two homeowners building the same ADU can have very different total project costs because of differences in utilities, site conditions, access, and local regulations."}, {"remember": "A successful ADU project begins with planning—not construction."}],
  m5c2: [{"p": "The first factor that determines your ADU location is your city's regulations. Local regulations determine where an ADU may legally be built. Setbacks, height limits, easements, lot coverage, and required separation from the primary home establish your buildable area—and these rules vary by municipality, so check with your local planning department."}, {"p": "Once you understand where an ADU can legally be placed, you can determine the best location on your property."}, {"h": "Your ADU location affects", "list": ["Water connection distance", "Sewer connection distance", "Electrical connection distance", "Trenching costs", "Privacy", "Backyard functionality"]}, {"remember": "The city determines where you can build. Your property determines what it will cost."}],
  m5c3: [{"p": "Every detached ADU requires utility connections. The farther the ADU is from the existing utilities, the greater the installation cost is likely to be."}, {"h": "Primary utility considerations", "list": ["Water", "Sewer", "Electrical service", "Trenching between the primary home and the ADU"]}, {"p": "In some cases, your electrical panel or water service may need to be upgraded to support an additional dwelling unit. Knowing where your utilities are located before construction begins will help you develop a more accurate preliminary budget."}, {"remember": "Utility location has a direct impact on pre-site costs."}],
  m5c4: [{"p": "Some properties require little site preparation, while others require additional work before construction can begin."}, {"h": "Potential site preparation may include", "list": ["Tree removal", "Grading", "Drainage improvements", "Retaining walls", "Demolition of existing structures", "Calling 811 before digging"]}, {"p": "Identifying these conditions early helps you estimate costs more accurately and avoid unexpected expenses during construction."}, {"remember": "Every property is unique. Understanding your site's conditions is an important step in developing a realistic budget."}],
  m5c5: [{"p": "Most detached ADUs are built on a reinforced concrete slab foundation designed to meet local building codes and engineered plans. A manufactured home may require a different type of foundation."}, {"p": "The foundation provides a stable base for the structure and is one of the first major construction costs in your project."}, {"h": "Typical slab foundation cost", "p": "Approximately $8,000 to $18,000 for many 600–800 square foot detached ADUs built on relatively level sites. Actual costs depend on your site conditions, engineered plans, and local requirements, so treat this as a planning range rather than a fixed figure."}, {"remember": "A properly designed foundation is one of the most important components of every structure."}],
  m5c6: [{"p": "Before construction begins, your ADU project must meet your city's requirements. Depending on your project and your municipality, this may include:"}, {"h": "Common requirements", "list": ["Property survey (if required)", "Building permit", "Trade permits (electrical, plumbing, and mechanical)", "Required inspections during construction"]}, {"p": "A property survey identifies your property's existing boundaries and improvements. It does not determine what you can build. After completing this course, the next step is verifying your property. The ADUAtlas Property Feasibility Study applies your local ADU regulations to your specific property, helping you understand what may be possible before spending significant time and money."}, {"remember": "A survey shows what exists. A Property Feasibility Study helps determine what may be possible."}],
  m5c7: [{"p": "Every ADU project follows a series of steps, and every project timeline is different. Seasons and weather can have a huge effect on timelines. A project can take anywhere from 8 months to 2 years."}, {"p": "Your local builder or city hall can give you a better idea before you start. Some cities take 2 months to approve plans and come out for their initial inspection; others take two weeks. Just ask so you are prepared. Expect estimates—no builder will give you an exact date."}, {"h": "Your timeline depends on factors including but not limited to", "list": ["Site preparation", "Permit approval", "Builder availability", "ADU type", "Weather"]}, {"p": "Planning ahead and completing each step in the proper order can help reduce delays. Rehabbers say most jobs will take twice as long and 50% more money than expected. ADU projects don't have as many unknowns, but things can still come up to either delay or speed up your project—delays are more common."}, {"remember": "Building an ADU is a process. Completing each step in the proper order helps keep your project on schedule."}],
  m5c8: [{"p": "A realistic budget includes more than the cost of the ADU itself."}, {"h": "Your preliminary budget should include", "list": ["ADU structure", "Foundation", "Utility connections", "Site preparation", "Survey (if required)", "Engineering", "Permits and inspections", "A builder or general contractor", "Contingency", "Delivery and setup"]}, {"p": "A preliminary budget helps you determine whether your project is financially realistic before requesting builder proposals."}, {"remember": "A complete budget helps reduce unexpected costs during construction."}],
  m5c9: [{"p": "Education teaches you the ADU process. The next step is applying that knowledge to your property."}, {"p": "The ADUAtlas Property Feasibility Study helps homeowners verify property-specific information before hiring a builder or purchasing construction plans. The study helps you better understand your property, evaluate your options, and make informed decisions before committing significant time and money."}, {"h": "The bigger picture", "p": "Building an ADU starts long before construction begins. Understanding your property's regulations, utility locations, site conditions, foundation requirements, permits, inspections, timelines, and preliminary budget will help you make informed decisions and avoid many common and costly mistakes. The path is simple to remember: Learn → Verify → Review → Plan → Verify → Build."}, {"remember": "Education prepares you for the process. A Property Feasibility Study helps apply that knowledge to your property."}],
  // ── MODULE 6: Module 6: ADU FAQ & Critical Planning Topics ──
  m6c1: [{"p": "Before diving into regulations, costs, and builders, it helps to ground yourself in a few fundamentals. These first questions set the stage for everything that follows and explain why an approach that works on one property may not work on another."}, {"h": "1. Is building an ADU the same everywhere?", "p": "Not at all. ADU regulations, construction costs, permit requirements, and approval processes vary by state, county, city, neighborhood, and sometimes even by a single address. Every project is influenced by factors such as state regulations, local zoning, property conditions, utility availability, site access, budget, and your personal goals. The exact same ADU may be legal on one property and prohibited on another only a few blocks away."}, {"h": "2. What is an ADU? What is a JADU?", "p": "An Accessory Dwelling Unit (ADU) is a secondary residential dwelling located on the same property as a primary residence, typically with its own utilities. It generally includes a kitchen, bathroom, living area, and sleeping area. A JADU (Junior ADU) is created inside the existing home with a separate entrance. Understanding this terminology helps you compare ADU types, communicate with builders, and interpret local regulations."}, {"h": "3. Why do rules vary by state and city?", "p": "Most states adopt building standards based on the International Residential Code (IRC). Cities and counties then determine how those standards are applied through local regulations such as setbacks, height limits, parking requirements, ADU placement, design standards, and utility requirements. Knowing which rules are set by the state and which are controlled locally can help prevent redesigns, permit delays, and unexpected costs."}, {"remember": "Every property, city, and homeowner is different. The same ADU can be legal on one lot and prohibited a few blocks away, so the right question asked at the right time can save thousands of dollars."}],
  m6c2: [{"p": "Regulations are where many expensive surprises hide. The answers below point you toward the right sources, but remember: rules change, and the only reliable confirmation comes from your local jurisdiction."}, {"h": "4. How accurate is zoning information on ADUAtlas?", "p": "ADU regulations change regularly. ADUAtlas updates its information frequently and provides links to official municipal resources whenever possible. Always verify current regulations on your city's website and with your local jurisdiction before purchasing an ADU, ordering plans, or beginning construction, because cities may update zoning ordinances, building requirements, or permit procedures without notice."}, {"h": "5. Can I use my ADU as a short-term rental (Airbnb)?", "p": "Maybe. Some cities allow short-term rentals, while others prohibit them or require a separate permit or license. An ADU may be legal while short-term rentals are not allowed, so always verify your local regulations before planning to use an ADU this way."}, {"h": "6. Will my HOA allow an ADU?", "p": "Sometimes not, and you probably already know how strict they can be. Some Homeowners Associations restrict detached structures, architectural styles, exterior materials, or other improvements. Review your HOA governing documents before moving forward, since understanding these restrictions early can prevent costly redesigns and delays."}, {"h": "7. How close can an ADU be to my property line?", "p": "Every jurisdiction is different. Many cities require side and rear setbacks in the range of roughly three to five feet, while separation from the primary residence may range from six to ten feet or more. These are general ranges only; always verify local regulations. Setbacks determine your buildable area and may affect the size and location of your ADU."}, {"h": "8. Can I build more than one ADU?", "p": "Some jurisdictions allow multiple ADUs, while others limit properties to one. Rules vary depending on state law, local ordinances, lot size, zoning, and whether the property contains a single-family or multi-family residence. Because regulations continue to evolve, understanding what is permitted today may reveal opportunities that did not exist just a few years ago."}, {"h": "9. Can I convert my garage into a JADU?", "p": "Often, yes. Garage conversions are common in many jurisdictions but remain subject to local zoning, building codes, and permit requirements. They may follow different regulations than detached ADUs, so verify your local requirements before beginning."}, {"remember": "Regulations are set at multiple levels and change often. Always verify current rules with your local planning department before you buy, design, or build."}],
  m6c3: [{"p": "Preparation costs far less than redesigns and delays. These questions focus on evaluating your specific parcel so you arrive at every later step informed."}, {"h": "10. Why should I complete an ADUAtlas Property Feasibility Report before speaking with a builder?", "p": "Builders cannot provide an accurate estimate without understanding what can legally and physically be built on your property. The Property Feasibility Report helps determine whether an ADU is permitted, maximum potential ADU size, setback limitations, preliminary placement options, property dimensions, utility considerations, and estimated pre-site costs. Arriving prepared allows builders to give more accurate estimates and makes proposals easier to compare."}, {"h": "11. Do I need an official survey?", "p": "Usually yes, although requirements vary by jurisdiction. A typical survey costs about $500 to $2,000 and takes roughly two to three weeks. Keep in mind that a survey identifies existing property features but does not determine whether your proposed ADU complies with local regulations. Many homeowners buy a survey before confirming their project is even feasible; understanding your property's potential first may save unnecessary expense."}, {"h": "12. What is the ADU Ready Score? What is the NAPE score?", "p": "The ADU Ready Score is an ADUAtlas planning tool that evaluates factors affecting the likelihood of successfully building an ADU, including lot characteristics, site access, utility availability, local regulations, and site conditions. Each factor helps identify opportunities and challenges before significant money is invested. NAPE, the National ADU Property Evaluation tool, can be completed using tax records and a visual of the property, making it a useful tool for a Realtor."}, {"h": "13. What happens if my property has an easement or another obstacle?", "p": "An easement, utility corridor, drainage area, protected tree, or other site obstacle may reduce or eliminate your buildable area. Ask your city whether they will allow the easement square footage to count toward minimum size requirements, and note that a utility easement may still be vacant space. Review your title documents, survey, and local property records (including your city's property tax lookup) before finalizing your ADU location. Some obstacles can be relocated or removed; others cannot."}, {"h": "14. What if my backyard is on a slope?", "p": "It depends on the degree of the angle, or the highest point relative to street level. Sloping properties often require grading, retaining walls, specialized foundations, and additional drainage improvements, all of which may significantly increase project costs. Site preparation is often one of the largest unexpected expenses in an ADU project, so understanding your property's conditions early leads to more realistic budgeting."}, {"remember": "Identifying site constraints such as slopes, easements, and survey needs early prevents costly redesigns and delays. Understand what your property can support before spending money."}],
  m6c4: [{"p": "The ADU structure is only one part of your total project cost. These answers help you see the whole picture, from hidden site expenses to financing and taxes."}, {"h": "15. How much does an ADU cost?", "p": "ADU costs vary depending on size, construction method, site preparation, utility connections, location, materials, and finishes. Typical projects include costs for the ADU structure, site preparation, utility connections, foundation, permits, engineering, delivery (when applicable), and landscaping. Many complete ADU projects exceed $100,000, while larger or more complex projects may cost significantly more. The ADU itself is only one part of your total project cost."}, {"h": "16. What expenses are usually not included in advertised prices?", "p": "Many advertised ADU prices include only the structure. Additional costs may include water, sewer, electrical, and gas connections, as well as foundation, excavation, engineering, survey, permits, delivery, crane costs, landscaping, and driveways and sidewalks. Two ADUs with the same purchase price may have dramatically different installed costs."}, {"h": "17. Will my property taxes increase?", "p": "Possibly, and they usually rise over time anyway. Property tax treatment varies by state and local jurisdiction. Consult your local assessor or a tax professional to determine how an ADU may affect your property's assessed value, and include property taxes when planning the long-term cost of owning an ADU."}, {"h": "18. Are grants or incentives available?", "p": "Sometimes. Programs vary by state, local government, available funding, and homeowner qualifications. Some communities offer grants or financial incentives to encourage additional housing. These funding programs often have limited budgets and may close quickly once funds are allocated."}, {"h": "19. Can I finance an ADU?", "p": "Usually. Financing options may include a home equity loan, a home equity line of credit (HELOC), a construction loan, a cash-out refinance, or specialized ADU financing programs. Availability depends on your lender, credit qualifications, and property. Understanding your financing options early helps establish a realistic project budget."}, {"remember": "The advertised price is rarely the installed price. Budget for utilities, site work, permits, taxes, and financing costs from the start."}],
  m6c5: [{"p": "Once you know what your property can support, the focus shifts to selecting the right product and the right builder. These questions help you buy carefully, compare fairly, and understand the construction process."}, {"h": "20. Can I buy a low-cost ADU online?", "p": "Yes, but proceed carefully. Some products are not IRC compliant, cannot be permitted in many jurisdictions, or advertise only the shell rather than a completed home. Always verify that the product can be legally installed where you live. An ADU built outside the US will be measured in metric, so be cautious of the conversion. Returning an ADU to an overseas manufacturer may be impossible, and if it sounds too good to be true, it usually is."}, {"h": "21. What does \"IRC compliant\" mean?", "p": "IRC stands for the International Residential Code. An IRC-compliant ADU is designed to meet residential building standards covering structural safety, electrical systems, plumbing, fire safety, and energy efficiency. Always ask the builder how their ADU meets or exceeds your state's IRC code, since IRC compliance is often required before permits can be issued."}, {"h": "22. Does an ADU increase my home's value?", "p": "Often, yes. However, the amount of added value depends on many variables, including location, ADU quality, the local housing market, rental demand, and property condition. An ADU may also provide value beyond resale by creating housing for family members or generating rental income. Every property is different, so consider value from both a financial and a lifestyle perspective."}, {"h": "23. Do ADUAtlas builders know local zoning?", "p": "ADUAtlas builders list the cities and ZIP codes they serve. National modular and manufactured home companies often design their homes to meet or exceed the highest building standards required by the states where they operate, and local builders are generally familiar with zoning in the areas they serve. However, regulations change frequently, so always verify current local regulations before purchasing an ADU or beginning construction."}, {"h": "24. How does ADUAtlas match homeowners with builders?", "p": "Builder profiles are searchable by city, ZIP code, ADU type, and builder service area. Builders may operate locally, regionally, or nationally. ADUAtlas does not provide builders with homeowners' contact information; you must reach out to them. Prepared homeowners have more productive conversations and receive more accurate estimates."}, {"h": "25. What information will builders need from me?", "p": "Builders typically need your property address, city ADU regulations, total project budget, ADU type, ADU size and square footage, lot size and dimensions, site conditions, utility information, and site access. The more information you provide, the more accurate the estimate. The ADUAtlas Course and Property Feasibility Report are designed to help you gather this before contacting builders."}, {"h": "26. Do I need separate utility meters?", "p": "It depends. Many ADUs share utilities with the primary residence, while others require separate services depending on local regulations and utility provider requirements. For water and sewer, requirements often depend on existing service capacity and the number of kitchens and bathrooms being served. Utility upgrades can become one of the largest unexpected costs in an ADU project."}, {"h": "27. How long does it take to build an ADU?", "p": "Planning, inspections, and permitting may take several months. Construction commonly takes 4 to 12 months depending on the construction method, location, weather, permitting, and project complexity. Building an ADU is a process, and proper planning helps reduce delays."}, {"h": "28. What inspections occur during construction?", "p": "Inspection requirements vary by jurisdiction but commonly include foundation, framing, plumbing, electrical, mechanical, insulation, and a final occupancy inspection. Inspections help verify that the ADU has been constructed in accordance with approved plans and applicable building codes."}, {"h": "29. How can I compare builder quotes fairly?", "p": "Request proposals with the same scope of work, matching ADU size, floor plan, finish level, foundation type, site assumptions, and utility work. Compare what is included before comparing the final price. The lowest price is not always the lowest total cost, and comparing equivalent proposals helps you make a more informed decision."}, {"h": "30. What mistakes do homeowners make most often?", "p": "The most common mistakes are buying before verifying regulations, underestimating utilities and pre-site expenses, missing site constraints, selecting the wrong ADU size, and failing to compare quotes fairly. Early planning avoids expensive redesigns."}, {"remember": "Verify a product can be legally installed where you live, insist on IRC compliance, and compare like-for-like scopes of work. The lowest price is not always the lowest total cost."}],
  m6c6: [{"p": "This final group clarifies the role ADUAtlas plays in your journey and answers the human questions about who can live in an ADU, whether you can sell it, and where the industry is headed."}, {"h": "31. How does ADUAtlas help prevent buying the wrong ADU?", "p": "By helping homeowners understand the full picture, including but not limited to state authority, local zoning, site planning, utility requirements, builder expectations, and ADU type education. The goal is to avoid purchasing something that cannot legally be installed."}, {"h": "32. Does ADUAtlas guarantee approvals or pricing?", "p": "No. Permits, costs, and timelines are determined by local governments and the professionals you hire. ADUAtlas provides planning guidance and guarantees only an ADU education and a feasibility study; those two things it does guarantee."}, {"h": "33. What does ADUAtlas not do?", "p": "ADUAtlas does not sell ADUs, act as a contractor, file permits, guarantee approvals, or guarantee pricing. It is an educational and planning platform that connects homeowners with builders and suppliers."}, {"h": "34. How long do I have access to the Property Feasibility Package?", "p": "Your package includes annual access to the builder database, video library, planning resources, and ongoing support materials. Because many ADU projects take months from research to completion, this ongoing access is designed to support you throughout the journey."}, {"h": "35. Can family members live in my ADU?", "p": "Yes. In fact, that is one of the primary reasons people build ADUs. Many homeowners build them for parents, adult children, caregivers, and other relatives."}, {"h": "36. Can I sell my ADU separately from my home?", "p": "Usually, no. Most ADUs remain legally tied to the primary residence and the property deed unless local laws specifically allow separate ownership."}, {"h": "37. How much is the ADU industry expected to generate in the next 5 to 10 years?", "p": "The ADU industry has experienced tremendous growth: roughly $13 billion five years ago, approximately $24 billion today, and a projected figure of about $50 billion within the next 5 to 10 years. This growth reflects increasing demand for additional housing and the growing acceptance of ADUs throughout the United States."}, {"h": "38. What are the advantages and disadvantages of living in a small ADU?", "p": "A smaller home offers many benefits but also requires compromises. Advantages include lower construction and operating costs, less maintenance, greater flexibility, and efficient use of space. Disadvantages include less storage, reduced living space, less privacy than a separate property, and the need for thoughtful organization. Choosing the right size depends on your lifestyle, goals, and intended use."}, {"h": "39. Did Elon Musk live in an ADU?", "p": "Yes. Elon Musk has publicly stated that he lived in a small, prefabricated modular home near SpaceX's Starbase facility in Boca Chica, Texas. He also owns a foldable prefabricated unit built by Boxabl that is used as a guest house. Small, well-designed homes are being embraced by everyone from first-time homeowners to some of the world's most successful entrepreneurs."}, {"h": "40. What is the process to save a homeowner the most money?", "p": "Follow the sequence: (1) determine what you can legally build, (2) learn the process, (3) verify local regulations, (4) understand what the property can support, (5) estimate pre-site costs, (6) explore ADU options, (7) complete a feasibility review, and (8) speak with builders using accurate information. Prepared homeowners make better decisions, receive more accurate pricing, and avoid many of the expensive mistakes that delay or derail ADU projects."}, {"remember": "ADUAtlas educates and plans; it does not sell ADUs, file permits, or guarantee approvals. Prepared homeowners who follow the process save the most money and make the most confident decisions."}],
  // ── MODULE 7: Module 7: False Starts — The National ADU Property Evaluation (NAPE) ──
  m7c1: [{"p":"One of the first questions almost every homeowner asks is: “Can I build an ADU on my property?” Sometimes the answer is yes. Sometimes the answer is not yet. And sometimes the answer is simply no. This module is designed to help identify those possibilities before you spend thousands of dollars on surveys, engineering, permits, or builder consultations."},{"h":"Many projects fail because homeowners discover too late that","list":["Their lot is too small","Required setbacks leave too little legal building area","A utility easement crosses the proposed building site","The property is located within a historic district","Utility upgrades make the project financially unrealistic"]},{"h":"Not every obstacle is permanent","p":"One homeowner was preparing to build an ADU when a plumbing review determined the existing water service would not support an additional kitchen and bathroom. The recommendation was to replace the water line from the home to the city main — an improvement estimated at approximately $25,000. It appeared the project was over. Then, a month later, the city notified the homeowner that the water service was already scheduled for replacement as part of a city infrastructure project. Because the work was already being completed, the homeowner was able to discuss installing a larger service line and adding a future connection for the ADU at the same time, potentially saving thousands of dollars. The same homeowner also asked the utility company to remove an old telephone pole on the property — removed at no cost."},{"p":"The lesson is simple: circumstances change, and sometimes obstacles have practical solutions. Ask questions, watch for planned city improvements, and explore every reasonable opportunity before assuming your project cannot move forward."},{"remember":"Sometimes the answer is yes, sometimes not yet, and sometimes no. The smartest homeowners discover potential problems early — before they become expensive mistakes."}],
  m7c2: [{"p":"Many homeowners assume that owning a property automatically means they can build an ADU. Unfortunately, that is not always true. Some projects fail because of legal restrictions. Others become financially impractical because of site conditions, utility upgrades, grading, retaining walls, or access limitations."},{"p":"In many cases, homeowners discover these problems only after spending thousands of dollars on surveys, engineering, architectural plans, or permits. Education before construction can save significant time, money, and frustration — and helping homeowners identify these issues before major financial commitments is one of the primary goals of ADUAtlas."},{"remember":"Owning a property does not automatically mean you can build an ADU. Identify legal and financial obstacles before major money is spent."}],
  m7c3: [{"p":"The National ADU Property Evaluation (NAPE) is an ADUAtlas planning tool developed to provide a fast, nationwide evaluation of a property's ADU potential. Using approximately 25 Yes-or-No questions, homeowners, home buyers, sellers, real estate professionals, investors, and builders can quickly evaluate whether there are any obvious legal, physical, or financial reasons an ADU project may not be practical."},{"p":"NAPE is not a permit approval, and it does not replace professional due diligence. Instead, it answers one important planning question: “Based on what I know today, is there any obvious reason I should not move forward?” Sometimes the answer is yes. Sometimes additional research is required. And sometimes discovering the answer is no before investing significant time and money may be one of the smartest financial decisions a homeowner ever makes."},{"remember":"The best projects begin with education — and sometimes the best outcome is knowing when to stop before you start."}],
  m7c4: [{"p":"Some property conditions create significant legal obstacles that are often difficult — or impossible — to overcome. While every property is unique, the following conditions frequently prevent an ADU from moving forward without major changes or approvals."},{"h":"Automatic no-go conditions","list":["ADU not permitted by local zoning — construction cannot proceed unless regulations change or a variance is granted","Minimum lot size requirement not met — many jurisdictions require a minimum lot size before an ADU is permitted","Minimum lot width or depth not met — a narrow or shallow lot may not provide enough room to legally construct an ADU","Required setbacks eliminate the buildable area — even when an ADU is permitted, setbacks may leave little or no legal building envelope","Historic district restrictions — preservation rules may prohibit new construction or require extensive review","HOA or deed restrictions — some associations and private deed restrictions limit or prohibit detached structures; verify before investing in design work","Utility or drainage easements occupy the buildable area — easements may prevent construction within portions of your property"]},{"p":"ADUAtlas tip: a “No-Go” today does not always mean “No” forever. Regulations change, utility projects occur, and variances may be available in certain situations. Verify your options before giving up on a project."},{"remember":"Automatic no-go conditions are legal obstacles — zoning, lot size and dimensions, setbacks, historic districts, HOA restrictions, and easements. Verify each before spending on design work."}],
  m7c5: [{"p":"The following conditions do not automatically prevent construction, but they often increase costs, delays, or construction complexity."},{"h":"Significant slope or challenging topography","p":"Steep lots may require engineered foundations, grading, excavation, retaining walls, or specialized drainage systems that can add tens of thousands of dollars to the project."},{"h":"Floodplain or environmental restrictions","p":"Flood zones, wetlands, conservation easements, coastal overlays, or protected environmental areas may require additional engineering, permits, or government approvals. Review your city's GIS maps, property tax records, and planning department resources to determine whether environmental restrictions affect your property."},{"h":"Protected trees or wildlife","p":"Protected or heritage trees may not be removed without approval, and certain wildlife habitats are protected under state or federal law — for example, federal regulations protect areas surrounding active Bald Eagle nests. Identifying these issues early may prevent costly redesigns later."},{"h":"Limited construction access","p":"Building materials and equipment must reach the construction site. Narrow side yards, fences, neighboring homes, overhead utility lines, steep terrain, or limited backyard access may require alternative construction methods or specialized equipment — and can substantially increase construction costs."},{"h":"Existing pool or large tree","p":"Sometimes the only practical location for an ADU is already occupied by a swimming pool or a mature tree. Removing or relocating these features can significantly increase project costs and should be included in your preliminary planning."},{"h":"Undersized water service","p":"Adding another kitchen, bathroom, and laundry room increases water demand, and older homes sometimes require a larger water service line before an ADU can be added. When your utility company locates underground utilities, ask whether your existing water service appears adequate — and if an upgrade is necessary, ask whether future city infrastructure improvements may reduce your costs."},{"h":"Septic system limitations","p":"Properties served by septic systems may require system upgrades before an additional dwelling can be approved. Because these improvements can be expensive, septic capacity should be evaluated early in the planning process."},{"h":"Major utility relocation","p":"Telephone poles, transformers, gas meters, electrical pedestals, and other utility infrastructure occasionally interfere with ADU construction. Some relocations may be completed at little or no cost, while others can cost thousands of dollars. Begin these conversations early — utility improvements already scheduled by your city or provider may reduce future expenses."},{"remember":"High-risk false starts — slope, environmental overlays, protected trees, limited access, undersized utilities, septic limits, utility relocations — don't stop a project outright, but they can change its cost dramatically. Flag them early."}],
  m7c6: [{"p":"Just because an ADU is legally permitted does not mean it is financially practical. A successful project requires both legal feasibility and financial feasibility."},{"h":"Site preparation costs","p":"Utility connections, trenching, retaining walls, grading, excavation, foundations, engineering, and site obstacles can dramatically increase the overall project cost. These expenses should be estimated before selecting an ADU design."},{"h":"The maximum legal ADU may be smaller than expected","p":"Local regulations may limit ADU size based on lot dimensions, setbacks, or a percentage of the primary residence. Some homeowners also overlook opportunities to build a second story where permitted. Understanding the maximum allowable size before selecting an ADU can prevent costly redesigns later."},{"h":"Rental income or resale value","p":"A legally buildable ADU should also make financial sense. Estimate potential rental income, future resale value, and long-term return on investment before committing to construction."},{"h":"Financing","p":"Even a well-planned project requires adequate financing. Review available equity, construction financing, HELOC options, or other funding sources before moving forward."},{"h":"Consider other ADU construction methods","p":"The custom stick-built ADU you originally envisioned may not be the most practical option. Modular, prefabricated, panelized, SIP, or kit homes often reduce construction time and overall costs while providing attractive, modern designs. Before making a final decision, review all available ADU construction methods presented earlier in this course."},{"remember":"A successful project requires both legal feasibility and financial feasibility. Estimate site preparation, verify the maximum size, and confirm financing before committing to a design."}],
  m7c7: [{"p":"The National ADU Property Evaluation provides homeowners with an objective way to evaluate whether a property appears to be a good candidate for an ADU before making major financial commitments. Each question is answered Yes or No; the final score — out of 100 possible points — helps identify whether additional research is needed before moving forward. The scoring system is an early planning tool, not a permit approval."},{"h":"1. Zoning & legal feasibility (30 points)","list":["ADU permitted by local zoning","Minimum lot size satisfied","Minimum lot width and depth satisfied","Maximum ADU size complies with local regulations","Height limits satisfied","Required setbacks satisfied","ADU size does not exceed local percentage requirements","HOA or deed restrictions reviewed","Historic district requirements reviewed","Minimum separation distance from the primary residence satisfied"]},{"h":"2. Lot & physical site conditions (25 points)","list":["Slope and topography","Floodplain or environmental overlays","Protected trees or habitat","Easements","Adequate buildable backyard area"]},{"h":"3. Utilities & infrastructure (15 points)","list":["Utility access available","Septic suitability, if applicable","Water, sewer, and electrical connection feasibility"]},{"h":"4. Site access & construction logistics (15 points)","list":["Emergency access","Backyard construction access","Crane or delivery access","Overhead utility conflicts"]},{"h":"5. Market & financial practicality (15 points)","list":["Reasonable site preparation costs","ADU size justifies the investment","Financing available","Rental or resale potential","Overall financial practicality"]},{"p":"You can score your own property with the interactive ADU Ready Score in your Report Packet — it follows this exact system."},{"remember":"NAPE scores five categories — zoning & legal (30), site conditions (25), utilities (15), access & logistics (15), and financial practicality (15) — out of 100 points."}],
  m7c8: [{"h":"Grade A — 90–100 points · Excellent candidate","p":"No significant legal, physical, or financial obstacles have been identified. Proceed with confidence and begin preparing your detailed feasibility review."},{"h":"Grade B — 80–89 points · Good candidate","p":"The property appears suitable for an ADU, but several items require additional investigation before moving forward."},{"h":"Grade C — 70–79 points · Proceed with caution","p":"Several factors require additional research. Complete a detailed feasibility study before making major financial commitments."},{"h":"Grade D — below 70 points · High-risk project","p":"Significant legal, physical, or financial obstacles have been identified. Carefully evaluate whether the project still makes financial sense."},{"h":"Grade F · False start","p":"One or more major project killers have been identified. Sometimes the smartest financial decision is recognizing when a project should not move forward."},{"h":"A “No” today does not always mean “No” forever","list":["Cities update zoning regulations","Infrastructure improves","Utilities are upgraded","Variances may be granted"]},{"p":"Continue asking questions and verifying your options before making a final decision. While NAPE is not a permit approval or a guarantee that an ADU can be built, it provides an objective framework for evaluating whether a project appears practical before moving to the next step. Sometimes NAPE confirms you're ready to move forward. Sometimes it identifies issues that require additional research. And occasionally, it helps homeowners avoid an expensive mistake altogether."},{"remember":"The goal is not simply to build an ADU. The goal is to build the right ADU, on the right property, with realistic expectations and a well-informed plan."}],
  // ── MODULE 8: Module 8: Builder Preparation ──
  m8c1: [{"p": "By now you understand what an ADU is, the regulations that may affect your property, the different construction methods available, realistic budgeting, pre-site preparation, and how to evaluate whether your property appears to be a good candidate for an ADU. Now it's time to prepare for one of the most important steps in the process — speaking with builders."}, {"h": "Many homeowners contact builders too early", "p": "They don't yet know what type of ADU they want, they don't fully understand their property's limitations, they don't have a realistic budget, and they aren't prepared to answer the questions every experienced builder will ask. The result is often confusing proposals that are difficult — or impossible — to compare."}, {"h": "Builders can only work with the information they receive", "p": "Builders prepare accurate proposals based on the information you give them. If important information is missing, estimates are often based on assumptions rather than facts. That can lead to unexpected costs, change orders, delays, and confusion later in the project."}, {"h": "Organize before you request proposals", "p": "Before contacting a general contractor, builder, or ADU supplier, spend time organizing your project information. The more complete your information, the more accurate a builder's proposal is likely to be. Builders appreciate working with homeowners who understand their property, have realistic expectations, and have already completed much of the preliminary planning. This preparation helps eliminate unnecessary meetings, reduces misunderstandings, and often results in more accurate pricing."}, {"h": "Tools built for this step", "p": "The ADUAtlas Feasibility Study and Pre-Site Preparation Worksheets were specifically designed to help you organize project information and prepare for these conversations. The better prepared you are, the more productive your conversations with builders will be — and the easier it becomes to compare estimates and make informed decisions."}, {"remember": "Builders build. ADUAtlas prepares homeowners so they can make informed decisions before committing significant time and money. The more information you provide, the more accurate a builder's proposal is likely to be."}],
  m8c2: [{"p": "Before preparing a proposal, most builders will ask many of the following questions. Having these answers available will help your project move forward more efficiently and helps identify potential issues before detailed design work begins."}, {"h": "Property information", "list": ["What is the property address?", "Have you completed an ADUAtlas Feasibility Study?", "Have you obtained an official property survey?", "Is the primary residence single-story or two-story?"]}, {"h": "Intended use", "list": ["How do you plan to use the ADU — rental income, a family member, a guest house, a home office, or something else?"]}, {"h": "ADU selection", "list": ["What type of ADU are you interested in?", "Approximately what size would you like to build?", "Have you selected a construction method?"]}, {"h": "Budget", "list": ["What is your total project budget?", "Will you be financing the project?"]}, {"h": "Property conditions", "list": ["Is the property flat or sloped?", "Lot square footage and dimensions", "Does the property have mature trees?", "Are there retaining walls?", "Is backyard access limited?", "Are there overhead utility lines?", "Are there other site obstacles that may affect construction?"]}, {"h": "Additional questions", "list": ["Do you belong to a homeowners association (HOA)?", "When would you like construction to begin?", "Is there a desired completion date?"]}, {"remember": "The answers to these questions give builders what they need to prepare a more realistic proposal — and help surface potential issues before detailed design work begins."}],
  m8c3: [{"p": "Every builder's experience, services, and scope of work are different. Never assume two proposals include the same services simply because the total price appears similar. Ask questions and request written answers whenever possible. Clear communication at the beginning of a project helps reduce misunderstandings later."}, {"h": "Builder experience", "list": ["Which ADU construction services do you provide?", "What services are not included?", "Have you completed similar ADU projects in my city?", "Have you previously obtained permits for similar projects?", "May I see photos of completed projects?", "May I speak with previous clients?"]}, {"h": "Licensing & insurance", "list": ["Are you licensed to perform this work?", "Are you fully insured?", "Can you provide proof of licensing and insurance?"]}, {"h": "Proposal & scope of work", "list": ["Will you provide an itemized written estimate?", "Does your proposal include site preparation?", "Does it include excavation and grading?", "Does it include utility connections?", "Does it include foundation construction?", "Will you assist with permits and inspections?"]}, {"h": "Project management", "list": ["When can construction begin?", "What is the estimated construction timeline?", "Who will be my primary point of contact?", "May I schedule periodic walkthroughs during construction?", "How are change orders handled?"]}, {"h": "Payments", "list": ["What payment schedule do you require?", "What milestones trigger payments?", "Are there circumstances that could increase the contract price?"]}, {"h": "Construction options", "p": "If you are still evaluating construction methods, ask whether the builder offers modular homes, prefabricated ADUs, or panelized or kit homes — and whether they can coordinate pre-site preparation and utility connections."}, {"remember": "The more information you gather, the easier it becomes to compare builders on an equal basis rather than simply comparing price."}],
  m8c4: [{"p": "Not every factory-built ADU is sold the same way. Some companies sell only the structural shell. Others provide a nearly completed home. Understanding exactly what is included before making a purchase can prevent costly surprises later. Ask for a written list of everything included — and everything excluded."}, {"h": "What's included?", "list": ["Is this a completed home or only a structural shell?", "Are cabinets included?", "Is flooring included?", "Are appliances included?", "Are interior finishes included?", "Are plumbing fixtures included?", "Is electrical wiring included?", "Is the HVAC system included?", "Are windows and exterior doors included?", "What type of insulation is provided?", "Does the purchase price include delivery?", "Does the purchase price include installation?"]}, {"h": "Building standards", "p": "Ask how the ADU meets or exceeds building code, whether it meets the applicable code requirements for your area, and whether engineered drawings are included. Also ask whether these units have been pre-approved in your city or state. Code requirements and pre-approval programs vary by municipality, so confirm the specifics with your local building department."}, {"h": "Site requirements", "list": ["What type of foundation is required?", "Does my property require special site preparation?", "Can the unit be delivered directly to my backyard?", "Will a crane be required?", "What minimum access width is necessary?"]}, {"h": "Utility connections", "list": ["Who is responsible for connecting water?", "Who connects sewer?", "Who connects electrical service?", "Who connects natural gas, if applicable?", "Which utility work must be completed before delivery?", "Are utility connections included in the purchase price?"]}, {"h": "Permits", "list": ["Who prepares the permit package?", "Who submits plans to the city?", "Are permit fees included?", "Will additional engineering likely be required by my local jurisdiction?"]}, {"h": "Warranty & customer support", "list": ["What warranties are included?", "Who performs warranty repairs?", "Is local customer support available after installation?"]}, {"h": "Pricing — ask what is NOT included", "p": "Before signing a contract, ask exactly what is not included. Common exclusions to confirm are delivery charges, crane costs, foundation construction, site preparation, utility connections, permits, engineering, installation, and interior finishes."}, {"h": "Final tip", "p": "If a price appears unusually low, ask exactly what is included. Some advertised prices represent only the structural frame or shell. Always compare the total installed cost, not simply the advertised purchase price."}, {"remember": "Always compare the total installed cost — not the advertised purchase price. A low headline number often reflects only the shell."}],
  m8c5: [{"p": "Obtaining three to five bids is standard practice for most construction projects. Request itemized proposals whenever possible so you can compare the same scope of work. If one proposal is substantially higher or lower than the others, ask why."}, {"h": "Where builders' assumptions differ", "list": ["Site preparation", "Utility work", "Excavation", "Foundation requirements", "Permits", "Engineering", "Materials", "Construction methods"]}, {"h": "Look beyond the bottom line", "p": "A lower price does not always represent a better value. Likewise, the highest proposal is not always the best choice. Compare experience, communication, services provided, warranties, timelines, and overall value — not just price."}, {"h": "The contractor's saying", "p": "Remember: Good. Fast. Cheap. Pick two. Understanding exactly what each proposal includes will help you make a more informed decision."}, {"remember": "Every proposal should be compared based on the same scope of work — not simply the bottom-line price."}],
  m8c6: [{"h": "What the ADUAtlas tools provide", "p": "The ADUAtlas Feasibility Study and Pre-Site Preparation Worksheets are educational planning tools designed to help homeowners prepare before contacting builders. They provide preliminary information about property characteristics, estimated maximum ADU size, utility locations, site considerations, and estimated pre-site preparation items."}, {"h": "They don't replace professionals", "p": "Your builder, surveyor, engineer, utility providers, and local building department should verify all project-specific information before construction begins. The purpose of ADUAtlas is not to replace these professionals. Our purpose is to prepare homeowners so they can have better conversations, ask better questions, compare proposals more effectively, and make informed decisions throughout the planning process."}, {"h": "Module summary", "p": "Congratulations — you've completed the Builder Preparation module. Building an ADU is much more than selecting a floor plan or requesting a construction estimate. Successful projects begin with education and preparation, and builders can only prepare accurate proposals when they receive accurate information."}, {"h": "What you accomplished", "p": "By completing the ADUAtlas Feasibility Study, organizing your project information, and understanding the questions builders are likely to ask, you are already better prepared than most homeowners beginning the ADU process. You also learned to ask builders detailed questions about their experience, licensing, scope of work, pricing, permits, construction methods, and project management — and, if you're considering a modular, prefabricated, or kit home, why it's essential to compare the total installed cost rather than the advertised purchase price."}, {"h": "Moving on to Module 9", "p": "Next is the ADUAtlas Feasibility Study, where your research becomes a personalized planning tool for your specific property. Instead of relying on assumptions, you'll begin working with property-specific information, including potential ADU placement, estimated maximum ADU size, utility locations, site considerations, and preliminary pre-site preparation estimates. Remember: a survey shows what exists; a feasibility study evaluates what may be possible."}, {"remember": "The better prepared you are, the better your project is likely to be — and the easier it becomes to compare proposals with confidence."}],
  // ── MODULE 9: Module 9: Property Feasibility Study ──
  m9c1: [{"p": "One of the keys to a successful ADU project is knowing if you can build, what you can build, and where you can build it. Many homeowners choose an ADU style or a floor plan before verifying their property, and they try to estimate project costs before understanding their pre-site conditions. The truth is, you cannot create a realistic budget until you know your pre-site estimate."}, {"h": "The questions that shape your budget", "p": "Before committing significant time and money, ask yourself where your ADU can legally be built, what your pre-site conditions are, and how far the utility connections are from the proposed ADU. The answers can have a significant impact on your total project cost. The structure itself is only one part of that total — a great deal happens before construction ever begins."}, {"h": "No two properties are alike", "p": "Two homeowners may build the same detached ADU in the same city yet have very different project costs because of differences in their property. Lot dimensions, setbacks, utility locations, existing structures, easements, and pre-site conditions all influence what may be possible."}, {"h": "What to verify first", "list": ["If you can build", "What you can build", "Where you can build", "Your pre-site conditions", "The distance from the utility connections to the proposed ADU"]}, {"p": "Without this information, you're simply guessing. Knowing these answers gives you the info a builder needs before you get a bid or quote, helps you create a more realistic budget, and lets you move forward with confidence. Because these facts depend on your local rules, always confirm zoning, setbacks, and fees with your local planning department."}, {"remember": "Verify your property before making major financial decisions."}],
  m9c2: [{"p": "The ADUAtlas Property Feasibility Study brings together the information homeowners need before hiring a builder. Instead of gathering details from multiple sources, it organizes everything into one planning packet designed specifically for homeowners."}, {"h": "Depending on your property, the study may include", "list": ["GIS Property Diagram", "Property dimensions", "Existing structure footprint", "Local zoning regulations applied", "Local zoning overlays", "Required setbacks", "Largest potential detached ADU placement", "Utility connection reference points", "Preliminary planning recommendations"]}, {"p": "Because zoning regulations, overlays, and setbacks vary by municipality, the study applies your local rules to your specific lot — but you should still confirm the details with your local planning department before finalizing plans."}, {"remember": "The Property Feasibility Study helps organize your project before construction begins."}],
  m9c3: [{"p": "The GIS Property Diagram provides a visual representation of your property with your local ADU regulations applied. Seeing everything laid out together makes it far easier to understand how your property and local rules work in tandem."}, {"h": "The diagram may show", "list": ["Property dimensions", "Existing home footprint", "Required setbacks", "Proposed detached ADU location", "Largest potential ADU placement", "Utility connection reference points"]}, {"p": "This allows you to better understand your options before meeting with builders. Keep in mind that setbacks and placement limits are set locally, so treat the diagram as a planning tool and verify the specifics with your local planning department."}, {"remember": "Seeing your property visually makes planning easier and helps you better understand your options."}],
  m9c4: [{"p": "The Property Feasibility Study includes interactive worksheets designed to help you organize your project and prepare for the next steps. They help you organize information, compare options, and make informed decisions before hiring a builder."}, {"h": "The four worksheets", "list": ["NAPE – National ADU Property Evaluation", "Pre-Filled Preliminary Budget Worksheet", "Builder & Supplier Comparison Scorecard", "Project Timeline Planner"]}, {"remember": "The worksheets help turn information into a practical plan."}],
  m9c5: [{"p": "The National ADU Property Evaluation, or NAPE, is designed to help homeowners identify opportunities and potential concerns before moving forward. It provides an overall evaluation of your property's detached ADU potential and helps identify areas that may require additional review."}, {"h": "The evaluation considers factors from throughout this course", "list": ["Local regulations", "Property size", "Existing improvements", "Site conditions", "Utility access", "Pre-site conditions"]}, {"p": "Whether you're building, buying, or selling a home, the NAPE provides valuable planning information before significant time and money are invested."}, {"remember": "The NAPE helps homeowners evaluate a property's detached ADU potential before moving forward."}],
  m9c6: [{"p": "The Property Feasibility Study also includes planning tools that help organize your project before construction begins. Combined with your pre-site estimate, these tools help you understand your total project before requesting builder proposals."}, {"h": "These tools help you estimate", "list": ["Preliminary project budget", "Project timeline", "Permit costs", "Inspection costs"]}, {"p": "Once completed, you'll have a much clearer understanding of your project and be better prepared to compare builder estimates. Permit and inspection costs are set locally, so confirm current fees with your local planning department as you refine your numbers."}, {"remember": "Planning first helps create a more realistic budget and project timeline."}],
  m9c7: [{"p": "Once you've completed the Property Feasibility Study and filled in the interactive worksheets, you can begin creating a realistic project budget and move forward with confidence. The worksheets organize your pre-site costs, estimated construction costs, permits, inspections, and project timeline into one planning package."}, {"h": "A better conversation with builders", "p": "Any reputable builder will verify your estimates and explain where their numbers differ — that's exactly what you want. Instead of starting with little or no information, you begin the conversation with a solid understanding of your property and your project. If you start with nothing, how can you expect to receive an accurate estimate?"}, {"h": "Why pre-site numbers matter", "p": "What if your pre-site costs are much higher than you expected? That would clearly affect your ADU budget. And if they're lower than expected, you may decide to build a larger ADU or invest more in finishes and upgrades. Either way, having this information before hiring a builder helps you make informed financial decisions."}, {"h": "Useful even if you're selling", "p": "The study can also be valuable when selling your home. Imagine telling prospective buyers the approximate detached ADU size your property may support, or sharing a completed NAPE evaluation. That information helps buyers better understand your property's potential."}, {"h": "What it would cost elsewhere", "p": "Some builders can provide much of this information as part of their planning process, but it often takes considerable time and may cost several thousand dollars. A survey company can supply important property information, but a survey alone does not apply local ADU regulations or evaluate your property's detached ADU opportunities. A traditional property feasibility study often costs $1,500 to $4,500, depending on the property and services provided. The ADUAtlas study brings this planning information together into one homeowner-focused packet."}, {"h": "Good. Fast. Cheap. Pick two.", "p": "There's an old saying in construction: Good, Fast, Cheap — pick two. Planning ahead allows you to decide which priorities matter most before construction begins. Education teaches you the process; the Property Feasibility Study helps you apply that knowledge to your property. Together, they help you make informed decisions before moving forward. Remember the path: Learn, Verify, Review, Plan, Verify, Build."}, {"remember": "ADUAtlas builds better-prepared homeowners. Education teaches you the process; the Property Feasibility Study helps you apply that knowledge to your property."}],
  // ── MODULE 10: Moving Forward Prepared: Putting It All Together ──
  m10c1: [{"p":"More than six years ago, I began exploring tiny homes while rehabbing a property in Grafton, Illinois. The property was located in a hollow surrounded by the river bluffs, about one minute from the Great River Road — a quaint, beautiful setting that seemed ideal for a tiny-home or glamping retreat. Three springs came down from the bluffs to a creek that emptied into the Mississippi. I named it Cedar Grove, because it was literally a cedar grove."},{"p":"I could see the finished project in my mind: small structures nestled into the landscape, visitors enjoying a peaceful river-town getaway, walking along the creek to the ice cream store or the Broken Arrow Tavern. Transforming that neglected property into something special was going to be a labor of love. The vision was exciting. The property conditions made it too expensive to do."},{"p":"Because the property sat along the side of a bluff, it required extensive excavation, grading, retaining walls, and steps. I spent approximately $30,000 on a retaining wall and stairs before realizing the remaining site-preparation expenses were beyond my available budget — and the city told me the Illinois Department of Conservation and Fish and Game had to approve any structure that crossed the creek. Sadly, I had to sell the property. The problem was not the vision. The problem was that I became committed to the vision before I fully understood the cost of preparing the property."},{"h":"That experience taught me several lessons","list":["The property must support the vision — the site, utilities, regulations, access, grading, drainage, and total budget must support it","Site preparation can change the entire project — difficult conditions can add tens of thousands of dollars before construction begins","Verification should come before emotional commitment — understand what can legally and realistically be built before attaching to a floor plan or vision","Knowing when to stop is part of good planning — sometimes the smartest decision is to revise, reduce, wait, choose another property, or stop"]},{"p":"ADUAtlas was created to help homeowners identify these issues earlier. The goal is not to tell you whether you should or should not build — it is to help you gather the information you need to make that decision for yourself. Honor the vision, but verify the potential expenses before committing to it."},{"remember":"The best time to identify an expensive site condition is before you spend significant time and money pursuing the project."}],
  m10c2: [{"p":"Your ADUAtlas Property Feasibility Study provides a property-specific starting point: a GIS-based property diagram, property dimensions, the existing structure footprint, preliminary zoning information, required setbacks, a possible ADU placement area, a preliminary maximum ADU size, potential utility locations and approximate distances, property risks and planning questions, a National ADU Property Evaluation, and the pre-site, permit, timeline, and inspection worksheets. It is the beginning of the verification process — your project may still require an official boundary survey, engineering, architectural plans, or final municipal approval."},{"h":"Once you receive your study, review it carefully and ask","list":["Does the property layout match what I see? Walk it","Are there fences, trees, structures, slopes, drainage areas, or access issues that may not appear in public data?","Are there easements, rights-of-way, or restrictions affecting the proposed location?","Are the utility locations correct — and is anything buried beneath the proposed ADU area?","Does the city interpret the regulations the same way?","Will the proposed ADU type fit the available space, with delivery and equipment access?","Does my budget support the site preparation and the structure?","What else should I verify?"]},{"h":"Verify the property","p":"Confirm property boundaries, setbacks, easements, physical conditions, access, and buildable space. An official survey may eventually be required, but the goal of preliminary feasibility is to identify obvious concerns before spending significant money on one."},{"h":"Verify the regulations","p":"Confirm the city's current interpretation of minimum lot size, maximum ADU size, setbacks, height, lot coverage, separation requirements, parking, owner-occupancy requirements where applicable, historic-district requirements, utility requirements, fire access, and permit procedures."},{"h":"Verify the ADU","p":"Confirm that the ADU type you are considering complies with local structural, energy, zoning, transportation, and foundation requirements — and if you are buying a property to build an ADU, look into the HOA rules. A low advertised price does not mean the structure is automatically legal or suitable for your property."},{"h":"Verify the budget","p":"Every estimate should eventually be reviewed by a qualified professional — choose your general contractor first. The worksheets help you begin; professional quotes help you confirm."},{"p":"And when speaking with any professional — city official, builder, utility provider, engineer, surveyor, plumber, electrician — always ask one more question: “Is there anything else I should know?” That question may uncover an issue you did not know to ask about."},{"remember":"The Feasibility Study gives you a starting point. Verification turns that starting point into a reliable project plan."}],
  m10c3: [{"p":"Utilities can be one of the most important and expensive parts of a detached ADU project. Your Feasibility Study may include a blue marker and a green marker near the proposed ADU location — preliminary planning points for water and sewer. They are not confirmed construction connection points. Arrange for utility locating professionals to verify the actual conditions and access points; ADUAtlas provides a professional contact for utility locating."},{"h":"Questions to ask during utility verification","list":["Is there anything buried beneath the proposed ADU location — or anything that would prohibit construction?","Are you marking only the utility line, or the actual connection point?","Where can the ADU connect to water, sewer, and gas?","Are there electric, gas, communication, drainage, abandoned, or unused lines nearby?","Are there easements or access areas that must remain open?","Will the proposed trench cross any existing utilities?","Who owns each line — and which work is the homeowner's responsibility versus the utility provider's?","What fees will the utility provider charge to make the connections?"]},{"h":"Water-service capacity","p":"A legal ADU adds another kitchen, bathroom, water heater, and plumbing fixtures. Your existing water service may or may not support the additional demand — ask the professional who marks the utilities. A homeowner can often find the size of the incoming line or meter, but a qualified plumber or utility professional should determine whether the system can support the ADU. Replacing a water line from the house to the municipal main can be expensive — investigate before finalizing the project. Ask: what size is my existing service, what size is required, will the meter or main connection need upgrading, is a separate meter required, who performs the connection, and what are the fees?"},{"h":"Sewer capacity and condition","p":"Ask whether the existing sewer can support the additional fixtures. A plumber may need to verify pipe size, condition, slope, connection point, distance, depth, backflow requirements, cleanout requirements, and whether a pump or ejector system may be necessary."},{"h":"Electric service","p":"Your existing panel and service may not support additional heating and cooling, water heating, cooking, lighting, appliances, laundry, or EV charging. Ask a licensed electrician whether the current service and panel have sufficient capacity — and if not, include service, panel, meter, trenching, and provider fees in your pre-site estimate."},{"h":"Kitchen and bathroom placement","p":"Builders frequently place kitchens and bathrooms near one another because they share plumbing. If these rooms sit on the side of the ADU closest to the primary house or utility access point, the project may need shorter trenches, less pipe, less excavation, fewer site repairs, and lower labor costs. Before finalizing the floor plan, compare the proposed kitchen and bathroom location to the utility connection points — a small change in orientation may produce meaningful savings. The floor plan can change the site budget: do not finalize the plan until the utility strategy has been reviewed."},{"remember":"Locate the utilities, confirm the connection points, verify capacity, and understand who is responsible for each part of the work."}],
  m10c4: [{"p":"Pre-site preparation is the work required to make the property ready for the ADU — completed before or alongside construction of the structure."},{"h":"Common pre-site expenses include, but are not limited to","list":["Survey, site plan, architectural or engineering work","Permit applications, plan-review, inspection, impact, and stormwater fees","Utility locating, water and sewer connections, electrical upgrades, gas connections","Trenching, excavation, grading, retaining walls","Tree removal, demolition, foundation","Delivery, crane, or equipment access","Temporary fencing, erosion control, drainage improvements","Driveway or walkway repairs, landscaping restoration"]},{"p":"Not every project will include every item — but every item should be considered. Some homeowners begin by choosing an ADU advertised at a specific price, then discover that site preparation adds tens of thousands of dollars. In difficult cases, pre-site work can add 30% to 50% or more to the total cost. That is why the Pre-Site Estimate Worksheet should be completed before final plans or contracts."},{"h":"Retaining walls, slopes, and grading","p":"Sloped properties may require excavation, soil removal, grading, drainage work, retaining walls, structural engineering, additional permits, special foundations, and equipment access. Requirements vary by city and wall height — a relatively small change in height can determine whether engineering and permits are required. A retaining wall roughly 30 feet wide and 15 feet deep, depending on height and site conditions, could cost well over $10,000. Do not estimate retaining-wall work on materials alone — obtain professional quotes that include the full scope."},{"h":"Permit and municipal fees","p":"Cities may charge application, plan-review, building-permit, trade-permit, inspection, sewer and water connection, stormwater, impact, address assignment, and certificate-of-occupancy fees. Some are based on project value, square footage, fixtures, or local formulas — use the blank lines in the worksheet to add expenses unique to your city."},{"h":"Utility-provider fees","p":"In many areas, your contractor completes work on the private-property side while the utility provider performs or approves the connection to the public system. There may be separate charges for meter installation, service upgrades, disconnects and reconnects, main taps, inspections, utility-company labor, and street or sidewalk restoration. Ask who performs each part of the work and obtain the related fees."},{"h":"A preliminary planning example","p":"One mid-size Midwestern example — not a quote, and not applicable to every property: site preparation and grading $1,500–$5,000 (~1–2%), utility trenching and connections $10,000–$25,000 (~5–10%), permits and local engineering $2,500–$8,000 (~1–3%), foundation $20,000–$40,000 (~10–15%), framing/finishes/construction $116,000–$272,000 (~70–80%), for an estimated total of $150,000–$350,000. Every project is different: a flat, accessible lot with nearby utilities may have modest site costs, while a sloped, constrained, or utility-challenged property may require far more. Pre-site preparation can range from roughly $12,000 to more than $100,000 — these numbers must be verified locally."},{"p":"The price of the structure could be as little as half of the total project. A $10,000 building kit advertised online is probably not a completed legal ADU — it may not include engineering, foundation, delivery, assembly, interior finishes, utilities, site preparation, labor, or code upgrades. Always compare the total installed project scope."},{"remember":"Complete the Pre-Site Estimate Worksheet before deciding how much you can spend on the ADU structure."}],
  m10c5: [{"p":"Your realistic project budget has two primary parts: the pre-site and site-related budget (the work required to prepare the property and connect the ADU) and the ADU structure and construction budget (the building, delivery, labor, finishes, systems, setup, and construction). Together they create the total all-in ADU budget. Do not determine the ADU budget first and then hope there is enough money left for the site — determine the preliminary pre-site budget first, then calculate what remains available for the ADU."},{"h":"Step 1 — Complete the pre-site worksheet","p":"Estimate every applicable line item, and do not leave a line blank simply because the cost is unknown. Mark it: quote needed, city verification required, utility fee unknown, engineer review required, builder confirmation needed. An unknown cost is still part of the project."},{"h":"Step 2 — Obtain professional estimates","p":"Contact the appropriate professionals for major or uncertain expenses: surveyor, civil or structural engineer, plumber, electrician, excavation contractor, concrete contractor, retaining-wall contractor, tree service, drainage contractor, utility provider, city permit office, and ADU builder. Obtain more than one estimate for major expenses when possible."},{"h":"Step 3 — Verify the scope","p":"A low quote may not include the same work as a higher quote. Ask: does it include labor, materials, permits, restoration, disposal, utility-company fees, engineering, and inspection corrections? What is excluded?"},{"h":"Step 4 — Add a contingency","p":"Existing properties frequently contain unknown conditions. Your budget should include a contingency appropriate for the project risk — discuss the amount with your builder, lender, and financial advisor."},{"h":"Step 5 — Determine your ADU budget","p":"After estimating the pre-site work, determine how much remains available for the structure, delivery, setup, interior finishes, appliances, heating and cooling, plumbing, electrical, exterior work, landscaping, and furnishings if included."},{"h":"Step 6 — Determine whether the project still makes sense","p":"Consider total project cost, available cash, financing, monthly payments, rental income if applicable, property value, family needs, timeline, future flexibility, and alternative options. The answer may be: yes, move forward; yes, but reduce the size; yes, but choose a different ADU type; yes, but wait; yes, but complete the project in phases; no, not on this property; or no, not within this budget. Any of these can be an informed decision."},{"p":"A common contractor rule: Good. Fast. Cheap. Pick any two. Good and fast will usually cost more; good and inexpensive will usually take longer; fast and inexpensive may require sacrificing quality. Understand your priorities before comparing proposals."},{"remember":"Determine the pre-site budget first, then what remains for the structure. An unknown cost is still part of the project — and any informed decision, including not building, is a successful outcome."}],
  m10c6: [{"p":"Builders appreciate prepared homeowners. A prepared homeowner understands that the builder's price for the ADU may not include every pre-site expense, that utilities, permits, engineering, site preparation, delivery, setup, and property conditions affect the final cost — and they are less likely to be shocked when the builder explains the total project scope. An uninformed homeowner may arrive expecting a completed ADU for the price of a small kit advertised online; that creates confusion for the homeowner and frustration for the builder."},{"h":"Organize your project folder","list":["Property Feasibility Study and GIS property diagram","NAPE Evaluation","Pre-Site Estimate Worksheet and preliminary total budget","Timeline, permit, and inspection worksheets","Builder Comparison Worksheet","Preferred ADU types and floor-plan ideas","Utility information and city correspondence","Property photos, questions, and notes"]},{"h":"Questions builders may ask you","p":"Be prepared to answer: how you plan to use the ADU, where you plan to place it, the size and construction type you're considering, your preliminary budget, whether you've completed a feasibility review and verified zoning, whether utilities have been located, whether the property is level or sloped, whether there is equipment and delivery access, whether there are easements, your floor-plan preferences, your desired timeline, whether you'll need financing, and which finishes matter to you. You may not have every answer yet — the goal is to know which answers are known, preliminary, or still need verification."},{"h":"Questions you should ask every builder","p":"Ask the same core questions so you can compare fairly: how many detached ADUs have you completed; may I see completed projects and references; are you licensed and insured; what construction types do you offer; what is included and excluded in the proposal; does the price include site work, utilities, foundation, delivery, and setup; who obtains permits, prepares plans, coordinates engineering, and communicates with the city; who is my primary point of contact and how often will I receive updates; what is the estimated timeline; how are change orders handled; what payment schedule is required; what warranties are included; what homeowner responsibilities remain; what additional expenses should I expect; and what could cause the price to increase?"},{"h":"Compare the same scope","p":"A proposal that appears less expensive may exclude work included in another. Compare structure, pre-site prep, foundation, delivery, setup, utilities, permits, engineering, interior finishes, appliances, heating and cooling, landscaping, cleanup, warranty, and timeline — line by line, using the Builder Comparison Worksheet."},{"p":"One more thing: builders build what they know. A builder usually recommends the construction methods and products their company offers, and may not have time to explain every ADU option available nationally. That does not make the builder wrong — it is one reason homeowners should learn the ADU universe before choosing a builder."},{"remember":"The better prepared you are, the more meaningful your builder conversations and proposals are likely to be."}],
  m10c7: [{"p":"Most city employees want to help homeowners when they can. But city departments are responsible for administering regulations, reviewing plans, issuing permits, conducting inspections, maintaining infrastructure, and protecting public safety — they do not usually have the time or resources to give every homeowner a complete ADU education. Arrive prepared: look up your city's regulations on its website first — minimum lot size, setbacks, height, maximum size, lot coverage, parking, fire access, utility requirements, historic-district rules, easements, permit applications, plan-review requirements, inspection schedules, fees, timelines, online permit portals, and any planned infrastructure work."},{"h":"Review old easements and rights-of-way","p":"Easements can affect the buildable area, and may appear in deeds, title records, surveys, utility maps, plats, and city or county records. I learned this personally when an electric utility easement affected roughly 10,000 square feet of my property — on paper, it reduced the usable lot area enough to threaten the minimum lot size required for an ADU. After discussing the situation with the city, I was given permission to move forward because the property conditions and purpose of the easement could be considered. That does not mean every city will reach the same conclusion — it means the first apparent obstacle may not always be the final answer. Ask the city how an older easement, right-of-way, or unusual parcel condition applies to your specific property."},{"h":"Planned infrastructure improvements","p":"Your city or utility provider may already have work planned in your neighborhood. In my case, an unused telephone pole was removed by the electric company at no cost, and the city's water and sewer department planned to replace infrastructure in my neighborhood within two years — by coordinating with the city and my plumber, I may be able to request a future connection while the system is open. Every city and provider has different policies, so don't assume the same accommodation — but ask: is any infrastructure work planned near my property? Are water or sewer mains scheduled for replacement? Are poles being removed or relocated? Is street reconstruction planned? Could upcoming work affect my timeline, reduce costs, or prevent duplicate work? Sometimes timing matters."},{"h":"Do not assume “No” before asking why","p":"A regulation, easement, setback, or property condition may appear to prohibit the project. Ask how the rule applies to your property and whether any formal review, exception, interpretation, or alternative placement is available. Do not expect an exception — but do not assume one is impossible without asking. Arrive with organized questions, take notes, and confirm important information in writing when possible."},{"remember":"Cities and utilities can provide valuable information, but prepared homeowners must ask clear questions and verify how the answers apply to their property."}],
  m10c8: [{"p":"Most homeowners hire a builder or general contractor to manage the project. Some decide to serve as their own general contractor. These are very different roles."},{"h":"Hiring a builder","p":"An experienced ADU builder may coordinate design, engineering, plans, permits, subcontractors, materials, scheduling, inspections, construction, change orders, quality control, and final completion. The exact scope depends on the contract — never assume a service is included; verify it in writing."},{"h":"Acting as your own general contractor","list":["Hiring licensed subcontractors, verifying insurance, reviewing licenses","Scheduling work, ordering materials, coordinating deliveries","Managing the construction sequence and timeline","Scheduling inspections and correcting failed inspections","Managing payments, reviewing invoices, approving change orders","Resolving disputes, protecting the property, maintaining safety","Keeping records, controlling the budget, communicating with the city"]},{"p":"Being your own general contractor can reduce some management costs, but it creates significant responsibility — daily decisions, follow-up, scheduling, documentation, and problem-solving. Choose this role only if it matches your experience, time, organization, risk tolerance, knowledge, communication skills, and ability to manage contractors and resolve problems."},{"remember":"Select the project-delivery method that matches your knowledge, time, budget, and ability to manage risk."}],
  m10c9: [{"p":"ADUAtlas was created because homeowners were making major decisions without first understanding the process. Builders sell and build what they offer. Manufacturers sell their products. Cities enforce regulations. Engineers engineer. Inspectors inspect. Utility providers maintain and connect infrastructure. ADUAtlas educates and prepares homeowners — we may be the only national resource dedicated to providing an unbiased ADU education and structured planning process before major financial commitments are made."},{"h":"One year of access","p":"ADU projects take time. You may complete the course today and revisit the information six months later — your goals may change, your budget may change, a regulation may change, a builder may recommend another option, or you may decide to wait. ADUAtlas provides one year of access so homeowners can learn at their own pace and revisit the information throughout the planning process."},{"h":"Our commitment to reliable information","p":"ADUAtlas is dedicated to providing solid, useful, and verifiable planning information. Property data, public records, mapping information, and local regulations can contain errors or change over time — homeowners must verify final project information with the appropriate city departments and professionals. If a purchased Property Feasibility Study contains a significant error that materially affects its usefulness, ADUAtlas will review the issue and, where appropriate under its published policy, provide a corrected report, replacement report, or refund. The purpose is not to promise that every preliminary conclusion will remain unchanged — it is to stand behind the quality and usefulness of the information provided."},{"h":"Your next steps","list":["Review your Feasibility Study — read every section, mark anything that requires verification","Verify utilities — locations, connection points, capacity, responsibility, and fees","Complete the Pre-Site Estimate Worksheet — every known, estimated, and unknown expense","Obtain professional quotes for major site and utility costs","Determine your total budget — pre-site + structure + construction + delivery + setup + permits + professional fees + contingency","Confirm the city process — permits, plans, fees, inspections, and expected timelines","Compare ADU options — the construction type that fits your property, regulations, goals, timeline, and budget","Interview builders — the same information and questions with each","Compare complete proposals — the same scope, not only the bottom-line price","Decide whether to move forward — only when you understand what you can build, where, what it requires, what it may cost, what still needs verification, and whether it meets your goals"]},{"remember":"Verify twice. Build once. And always ask one more question: what else? Is there anything I am missing?"}],
  m10c10: [{"p":"You began this course with a vision — a home for aging parents, a place for adult children, rental income, a guest house, a home office, more flexible living space, or a long-term investment. The vision matters. But the property, regulations, budget, utilities, construction method, and site conditions determine whether the vision can become reality."},{"h":"Throughout this course, you learned","list":["What a detached ADU is and why homeowners build them","How state and local regulations work","The ten-step ADU process","More than 1,000 ADU options and how construction methods differ","Why pre-site preparation matters","The questions homeowners frequently ask","How to identify false starts","How to prepare for builders","Why property feasibility matters — and how to move forward prepared"]},{"p":"When you began this course, building an ADU may have seemed like a matter of choosing an ADU, finding a builder, and comparing prices. You now understand the process, the questions, and the choices: why the property must be verified, why site preparation matters, why the advertised price of an ADU is not the total project cost, why builders need accurate information, and why the city, utility providers, engineers, designers, builders, and inspectors each have a different role."},{"p":"You may decide to move forward immediately. You may revise your plan, choose another ADU type, adjust the size — or decide the project does not make sense. An informed decision is a successful outcome. Most importantly, you now know how to determine whether building an ADU makes sense for your property, your budget, your goals, and your vision."},{"p":"Remember the ADUAtlas framework: Learn → Verify → Review → Plan → Verify → Build. Measure twice, cut once. Verify twice, build once. Building an ADU is a process — not a purchase."},{"remember":"ADUAtlas builds better-prepared homeowners. An informed decision — whichever way it goes — is a successful outcome."}],
};

